"Sam" (sam@greenaum.demon.co.uk) posted --

>>             ....Moving the cursor, scrolling the screen, opening menus,
>> navigating through those menus -- stuff like that.
>
> Everyone uses mice for that now though, control keys are quick
> shortcuts for a few common tasks, and emergency backup for when your
> mouse clogs up.

"Everyone" is given no choice. The Ctrl key didn't exist on the first
Macs, it did next to nothing in DOS WordPerfect, and it did *nothing*
-- it was not even used for so-called shortcuts -- in Windows
applications until IBM moved it out of the home row.

Do you think people were clamoring for the industry to disable the Ctrl
key in the mid eighties? "Hey, we don't want that key to do anything.
Kill it. We like inactive keys." Of course not -- but they accepted an
inactive key out of ignorance and unthinking faith in big business. Big
business that saw an opportunity to create a carrot on a stick....

> Damn those function keys!

My sentiments exactly. And the arrow, paging, and editing keys. I
wouldn't outlaw them; I would outlaw making them indispensable.

> Anyway, I once used a VDU-entry program, designed for people to type
> data in off forms for hours continuously, and the keyboard was rigged
> with all sorts of strange functions to make it faster, like you didn't
> need to press enter to end a line, and the QWERTY section became a
> numeric keypad when you had a numeric field, etc. It was a nightmare.

No one said every keyboard UI is a good keyboard UI.

> Eurgh! Come on, Wordstar was obsolete ten years ago. Nowadays it's not
> just typists and programmers, who can spend weeks learning all the
> little intricacies, that use keyboards. People want to be able to
> press a key and know what it does, without having to go on a course.
> And most people can't touch-type anyway, and have no need to.

1. The keystrokes I mentioned are not WordStar keystrokes. They are
   ANSI, ECMA, and ISO keystrokes. They are part of Unicode. They work
   in countless applications.

2. What do you mean by obsolete? In terms of formatting capabilities,
   yes; in terms of the means of operation, just the opposite. Who has
   to "go on a course"? The available keystrokes and their functions
   are all listed on the screen, the keystroke help changes
   dynamically, and it can be hidden when you don't want it.

   Apparently it is choice that is obsolete.

   Before you talk about WordStar, please give these pages a read:

http://ourworld.compuserve.com/homepages/sawyer/wordstar.htm
http://listserv.acsu.buffalo.edu/cgi-bin/wa?A2=ind9702c&L=wpcorp-l&O=T&P=3412

3. We should drag professionals down for the sake of non-professionals?
   Because most people can't touch-type, those who can must work
   inefficiently, and once anyone who can't learns how -- hardly anyone
   doesn't want to learn touch-typing, especially these days -- they
   must be prevented from fully exercising their skill? When allowing
   them to exercise their skill would in no way interfere with the
   needs of those who don't have that skill? Surely you jest.

