Joe Buck (jbuck@synopsys.com) wrote in news:5mnlsc$doi@hermes.synopsys.com:

> Not true: Microsoft applications use Ctrl a great deal for keyboard
> acceleration, and do so in a consistent way.

Yeah, the on-screen keystroke help is great, too, and I really like being
able to change the help level. Plus you can keep your hands in typing
position all the time, navigate through documents and menus using the
same main-block commands, and access all the most important and
frequently-used functions -- left and right cursor movement by character
and by word; delete, backspace, and on-screen help; and file, block, and
search operations -- using home-row keys. And isn't it great that these
functions are all on universal ASCII keystrokes so you can use similar
techniques on any system's keyboard just as long as it has Ctrl and the
ASCII character keys?

(I especially like the way Alt works: think about doing something, hold
Alt down for four seconds, change your mind, release Alt, and two seconds
later the File menu becomes active. Hold Alt down for six seconds and
release it, and File becomes active three seconds later. Very logical.
Very helpful. Anyone got an explanation for that?)

In no endeavor is Microsoft more consistent than in keeping people's
hands out of typing position and firmly on a pointing device or vendor-
specific keys. When Windows 1.0 came out, Ctrl was universally in the
home row, and not one of the application programs bundled with Windows
used a single Ctrl-key combination. Think about that: twenty-six command
keystrokes sitting less than an inch from your fingertips, six more an
inch and a half away, and not one had any function at all in the entire
application suite. A supremely ergonomic, universally available feature
that allowed unprecedented efficiency, completely disabled, just like
that. There was one exception: the main command shell, called MS-DOS
Executive, let you change disk drives with the Ctrl key. If you had three
drives you had three -- THREE -- working Ctrl-key commands.

(The program called Terminal was interesting. Comms programs normally
pass Ctrl-key combinations through and use Alt-key combinations, function
keys, etc., for local functions. Windows Terminal used Ctrl plus the
function keys for local functions, presumably so it could pass all the
non-Ctrl-shifted function-key commands MS wanted everyone to use.)

Consistency? Cut/copy/paste in Windows was originally Del/F2/Ins; later
it became Delete/Ctrl+Ins/Shift+Ins, and still later -- after IBM moved
Ctrl to where the Mac's Command key was -- it became Ctrl+X/C/V. It had
been Command+X/C/V on the Mac since that machine first appeared, a year
before Windows came out and two years before IBM moved Ctrl.

In news:338d9ac5.1812450@news.wam.umd.edu, Rob Rodgers
(rsrodger@wam.umd.edu) wrote --

> I would note that Windows, unlike the alternatives, allows full
> functionality from the keyboard.  Not X, not the Mac, not NeXT, &c.
> The keyboard support in Windows is one of the very, very few
> longstanding points in its favor.

Keyboard support in Windows is a cruel joke.

Long before I had any idea how powerful Microsoft was getting, before I
had any inkling of their business practices, before I had any reason to
install more than 640K, before I ever heard the word "bloat" applied to
software, before I noticed "MHz" appearing in every computer ad and every
discussion of CPUs, I gazed upon Windows 1.0 and knew I had met my enemy.
The reason was the keystrokes, and everything I have seen in the past
eleven years has confirmed what I felt then.

Dan Strychalski
