In article <01bc6d69$45c0ace0$efa43ccf@bobwyman>, "Bob Wyman"
<bob_wyman@msn.com> wrote:

> John Price <John_Price@chiinc.com> wrote in article
> <338EE4C7.41C6@chiinc.com>...
> > 2) If Dec *didn't* sue over these pattents, they would have lost them.
> > By my understanding of patent law, if you don't enforce your patents you
> > loose them.
> 
> There is no obligation to "enforce" patents once they are granted and you
> don't lose them if you don't go after folk who infringe them. You may be
> thinking of trademarks. You do have to enforce trademarks.

Wrong. If you fail to enforce a patent against a company, this can be used
as a defense at a later date.

> 
> Technology history is full of stories of "submarine patents" that have
> quietly sat on the shelf for years while people infringed them. Then, once
> a nice healthy batch of folk folk are using the technology, the patent
> holder pops up and says: "Surprise! You owe me money!". It is legal. 

Only if you can demonstrate that there was a good reason why you didn't
enforce the patent (you didn't know about the infringement, etc). The law
requires you to diligently enforce your patents when you know of
infringement or you lose the ability to enforce them. The patent would
still be valid, in that case, but unenforceable.

