  IBM chose Intel as their supplier for processors, for whatever reason is
irrelevant...


  IBM filed suit against Columbia, one of the first PC clone
manufacturers, contending Columbia violated IBM copyrights.  In a nutshell
IBM lost, the barn door was thrown open and everybody and their kid
brother made PC clones.

  Microsoft and Intel owe their current success to competitive development
of PC clones, since

        A) Intel was able to sell many more processors
        B) Microsoft was able to sell MS-DOS freely, clearly
           further evidence IBM didn't take the PC seriosly.
        C) Intel and Microsoft were free of restraint by IBM
           to further develop their product lines along with
           clone makers.

  If IBM had protected their design and software, particularly by
obtaining exclusive rights to MS/PC-DOS, the landscape would appear much
different.


  Now as to my assertion of "luck", or good fortune if you prefer,
it is improbably that Intel and Microsoft could have forseen the victory
by Columbia.  Granted they could position themselves for such a future,
but only with as much leeway as IBM would grant them.  Which was
apparently a lot.

