On Thu, 29 May 1997 18:13:03 -0400, Alberto C Moreira <amoreira@nine.com> wrote:
>
>But hey, using the mouse is great. It totally replaces entering commands 
>from the keyboard. 

Yeah, but do you touch-type, really fast?  I'm not necessarily disagreeing
with you here...using the keyboard just for text entry, and picking stuff
off of menus/buttons/whatever for everything else is great for a beginner,
and evidently for some experienced people.  But the irritation of taking
my hands (or at least one) off the keyboard/home row, grabbing the mouse,
doing whatever clicking is needed, and going back to what I was doing is
generally unacceptable, too much of a break in continuity.  Especially if
it's just for a silly thing like saving a file, cutting/pasting text, or
evaluating Lisp expressions. :)

I do most of my "real work" in X-based environments, and use emacs for
anything remotely resembling text-editing (coding, html, mail, etc.).
Sure, I could pick just about everything off of menus/buttons/etc. but
the keyboarding is much, much more convenient.  I gather that MS-Windows
doesn't use Ctrl for much, mainly relying on Alt to generate the extra
bits.  Unix apps tend to use just Ctrl (more likely to be found on a
terminal), or both (although most Motif accelerators use Alt).  So I
certainly couldn't live without Ctrl.  Please, no "Windows vs. Unix"
flames, anyone.

Anyway, if the argument is that Ctrl is superfluous in MS environments,
that doesn't bode too well for the extra Windows keys either. :) And
just think of the horrible consequences for 3D game fans if Ctrl
suddenly vanished.  In any case, many, many people do use it for
useful things, it's hardly a dinosaur.

  Mike




