dski@cameonet.cameo.com.twx (Dan Strychalski) writes:

> Ctrl-H/J/K/L is fine; I personally prefer Ctrl-E/S/D/X. Some of the other
> arrangements I've seen in Unix, like Ctrl-P/N/B/F and Ctrl-Y/V/who-knows-
> what, are the products of sick minds. What I want to know is why no
> program uses something like Ctrl-U/J/K/N, a nice, tight, mnemonic diamond
> that still leaves Ctrl-H for Backspace, Ctrl-I for Tab, and Ctrl-M for
> Return.

Dan, some people remember things better by their position (wordstar
and its Ctrl-E/S/D/X) but others remember things better by mnemonic
(Ctrl-P/N/B/F: Previous/Next/Back/Forward).  You are obviously
of the first type -- so am I (last time I moved I memorized the new
phone number by the patern of the digits, not the digits themselves :-).
But I remember trying to teach wordstar to someone of the second type.
They could not understand the relationship between position on the
keuboard and desired action.  They only wanted to know what S stood for 
as there is no S in LEFT.

// marc



