>Actually, I may be wrong, but I heard that the embedded CPU is by far the 
>larger if one considers shipping volumes.

Yes.

>If this is true, microcontroller programmers might not be a minority.

No.

A recent internal survey at a large telecom company found that 1/3 of
engineering time was spent on writing firmware for embedded processors,
particularly programmable DSPs.  Compilers for fixed-point programmable
DSP processors still suck, I'm afraid.  So there certainly is a lot
of engineering effort.

However, most of the time a given program is written once and then runs
in hundreds of thousands, or even millions, or processors containing
identical ROMs.  So while microcontroller and DSPs greatly outnumber
ix86 processors, for programmers it is the other way around.



