<eslinger@rudolf.nscl.msu.edu> writes:

>MIT superkeyboard that I read about in the Jargon File, no less than 7
>different activating keys, (ctrl, alt, shift, meta, top, left, right) and a
>suite of applications to utilize them.  kind of out of fashion now except for
>the keystroke thing (emacs anyone?)

Well, it was originally the Stanford AI Lab keyboard; if you read the Jargon
file a bit more, you'll see that in the early days, MIT hackers would type
altmode-altmode-cokebottle, while SAIL hackers would type C-M-T-S-cokebottle.

LispMs added a SUPER or HYPER (depending on Symbolics vs. Lisp Machines Inc./
Texas Instruments) key, probably the predecessor of the ALT key (though that is
obviously also a PC thing).

The LEFT and RIGHT thing is an X (and possibly, previously, a LispM) innovation
that assigns potentially different meanings to the 5 shift keys, not keys in
and of themselves.

I miss my SAIL account.  At least I've still got 36 bits to play with...
