Distribution: world
References: <m0wStLZ-000I15C@ivan.iecc.com> <3385F569.41C6@nas.nasa.gov> <1997May28.023046.1@eisner> <EAyv0n.AvM@mv.mv.com> <5mlc9e$ah6@tooting.netapp.com>
Organization: I speak ONLY for myself. Nobody else, no organization.
Reply-To: Everhart@Arisia.GCE.Com
Newsgroups: comp.arch,comp.os.vms,comp.sys.dec



Galaxies is a VMS technology. Not NT, not unix, not anything else.

You get VMS and when VMS supports Galaxies ("should be soon":)
you get the 10x performance with suitable hardware. Some of the
suitable hardware exists now, and the 10x performance presumes
a large configuration.

The factor 10 comes about because VMS can scale its performance
with this technology better than anything else. Thus if all you
have is a few CPUs, the advantage will be less. However, even a
handful of processors will have some advantages. 

Measuring relative performance to MVS on a 390 or the like will of
course be trickier since it's not the same iron.

As I've posted before, one of the keys is a distributed lock manager
used by the file system. There are several others but since I don't
have the text of the DECUS talks in front of me I will be cautious
in this forum.

Consider also the truth of the following: when you have an SMP box, if
a processor fails, you must bring the whole system down or lose data
integrity. That's not true of a cluster. Even if anyone knew how to do
a 100-way SMP box, what can be concluded about MTBF?

Pity more folks didn't get to the DECUS symposium.



