Tim May wrote:
> 
:
> > In article <5ldtsc$itr$2@excalibur.flash.net>, RokNroB@Flash.Net (RokNroB)
> > wrote:
> ...
> > > Wasn't the iAPX 432 modeled after IBM mainframe's and minicomputer's.  If I
> > > remember
> > > correctly, even the data book stated this!
:
> > Or, more seriously, maybe what was meant was AS/400. But certainly current
> > AS/400 is based on PPC and presents its VM image through software not
> > hardware.
> 
> I assume he was referrring to the System/38, a "capability-based"
> architeture IBM was selling in the late 70s and early 80s. I think any
> connections between the 432 and the System/38 are tenuous.
> 
> The System/38 was eventually folded into the AS/400 line, if I remember
> correctly.

I don't have it in front of me, but the standard reference
for all this is Levy's book on capability-based computer systems.

I just thought I would add that I, for one, am not convinced
that capability-based systems are dead, and I don't think
the iAPX 432 is necessarily proof that there was anything
wrong with the capabilities part.  The 432's variable
instruction encoding and many other CISC'y architectural
decisions on the 432 are orthogonal to the capability-based
addressing that made the processor "interesting".

In fact, I think what "Java" and similar systems are 
desparately in need of a "simple-but-sophisticated" 
capabilities-based security model that would allow 
untrusted and untrustworthy downloaded applications
to get granted exactly the privileges they need, and
no more.  [Somebody, somewhere must already be working
on this, I assume.]


