In alt.folklore.computers, eslinger@rudolf.nscl.msu.edu (Eric Eslinger) wrote:

>> Why is
>> backspace in the top right corner a good idea? Why not near the space
>> bar or instead of carraige return? Because people are used to it.

> my roommate has a compaq with a "special" compaq  keyboard that has the
> spacebar split in two and the left half of it is actually backspace.

I hate those, as did the owner of the keyboard I was using which was
designed that way.  I hit spacebar with either thumb, but often I hit it
with my left hand, especially when writing code or commands on a command
line.  When writing messages like this, I hit it most often with my
right hand.  Perhaps its a left-brain right-brain thing.

I've become used to the position of the control key on most keyboards,
but I've seen and used some keyboards where it is too small or there are
more keys to the left of it.  I've only ever used the right control key
in games.  Same with option/alt and command keys.  Before, I was used to
the Apple II series keyboard layouts.

>dvorak keyboard.  Now there is a good idea.  why aren't they more
>standard?  I mean really, it would make a lot of things a lot easier.
>hey, I have convinced myself.  I am going to go out and learn to type
>dvorak, save my wrists for future use.

I tried it myself.  Even tried changing my keycaps around for that
arrangement on my Apple IIgs keyboard.  That was doomed to failure since
not all the keycaps on that keyboard were of the same height or even of
the same post orientation.  Strange since there was hardware support on
the machine for the Dvorak layout.

My next keyboard will definitely be more ergonomic.

