"AST" (josako@astsoft.com) wrote --

> Ideally, it should be possible for the user to choose from a set of
  configurations or reset them to their liking, as EMACS does. Ideally, it
  should also be possible for non-programmer to learn how make these changes
  quickly and easily, unlike EMACS.

Well said. This non-programmer has had the same thought many times.

I must say this: Any such set of configurations would have to be for
real. I tried EMACS' "WordStar" configuration and went straight back to
JOE with jstarrc. EMACS' "WordStar" mode might have a superb keyboard UI;
it just shouldn't be called WordStar mode. JOE with jstarrc is more
WordStarry than WordStar. It is a work of art.

An easy-to-use keyboard reconfiguration utility might be hard to do --
changing the keyboard through the keyboard -- but I'm convinced it isn't
impossible. WordPerfect/DOS has the beginnings of such a routine.
WPCorp's idiotic command prompts and brain-dead macro editor ruin it more
than anything else -- except maybe the fact that if you just *look* at
the key bindings and immediately abort or exit, the program asks, "Save
changes?" That takes talent.

I changed Word's key bindings, too. Took all day and wasn't worth it.

I suspect it might be possible to write a utility that could, with
reasonable reliability, find a program's keystroke table and at least
change the arrow keys into Ctrl-key combinations. If anyone writes such a
utility, I will be your slave forever....

Dan Strychalski
