Marc Hyman (marc@dumbcat.codewright.com) wrote --

> Dan, some people remember things better by their position (wordstar
> and its Ctrl-E/S/D/X) but others remember things better by mnemonic
> (Ctrl-P/N/B/F: Previous/Next/Back/Forward).  You are obviously
> of the first type -- so am I (last time I moved I memorized the new
> phone number by the patern of the digits, not the digits themselves :-).

That might be so -- but I would be very surprised if anyone still had
problems once the full logic of a particular keyboard UI was explained to
them. Even I wished for a greater number of letter mnemonics when I
started with WordStar, but once I understood the whole picture -- which
took a while, because it wasn't explained the way it should have been,
either on screen or in the manual -- everything made perfect sense
(except ^T and ^Y, which I *still* think should have been reversed).

An overall grasp of something does wonders. I remember one guy who was
floundering badly in WordStar until I explained to him that characters,
spaces, line breaks, and everything else existed as a one-dimensional
string of numbers in the computer, which was why, for example, deleting a
line break was the same as deleting a letter. Something clicked in his
head, and all of a sudden he started tapping away like a pro.

I recently posted a complete one-screen map of the WordStar keyboard,
showing its three basic functional zones (one of which does use letter
mnemonics to good effect), on the ASCII art mailing list. A couple of
people wrote, "I wish I'd had that when I had to use WordStar."

Not that I object to the idea of letter-based mnemonics. If I'd felt I
had time to adjust, I would have tried the Ctrl-U/J/K/N cluster when I
remapped WordPerfect's keys. That seems like an easy-to-remember
compromise, but I've never seen it used anywhere. Ctrl-P/N/B/F makes
sense for beginners and occasional users, but one doesn't remain a
beginner very long, and occasional users are a tiny, fast-disappearing
minority; besides, it means constantly switching hands, reaching into the
area between the hands, and stretching between ^P and ^B. I can't see it
at all! I'd be curious to know if any dyed-in-the-wool, Ctrl+QWERTY-or-
nothing power typists prefer Ctrl-P/N/B/F over all the other options.

Dan Strychalski
dski at cameonet cameo com tw



