rbean@earth.execpc.com (Ron Bean) wrote:

>There was a Sinclair laptop (Z8?) that had *rubber* keys. It was
>pretty wierd, but I think I could have gotten used to it (I
>decided not to buy the machine for other reasons).

Cambridge Z88, Clive Sinclair invented it. The keyboard is a black
rubber mat with the keys moulded into it, you get a travel of about
2mm. It's actually very usable, I can touch-type on it and probably
get about 50wpm out of it. The keys are all full size, even the
cursors and shift, so it's much easier than any PC laptop I've seen.

It's also good to use, fast, efficient, 20 hour battery life from
Alkalines, blah blah.

It's not original, though, some of his earlier computers had rubber
keyboards, the Spectrum was the best-selling micro in the UK and
probably Europe, and there's more emulators for it than any other
platform. It used a *very* strange method of keyword-entry, with 2
types of shift key, 4 keyboard "modes", and a single keypress to
generate an entire BASIC keyword. There were about 200 different words
and symbols you could produce, from 40 keys.


