I think Merced is the key here. If it truly is based on VLIW technology or
some variant of VLIW+RISC, then there is a real possibility that it will be
signifigantly faster than any Alpha. It is instructive to note that TI's
TMS320C6x DSP uses a form of VLIW technology to offer 10 times the
performance of any other DSP. It runs at 200Mhz and uses only 550K
transistors. With the huge transistor count available to HP/Intel and .25
or .18 micron processes, the rumors of a 1000Mhz or faster VLIW cpu with
x86 and PA-RISC compatibility could really be true. HP already claims a
500-1500% performance increase over today's PA-RISC chips.

That is a lot of "if's" but if Alpha loses the performance crown it has
ZERO business case.

