In article <5mnfpv$d9m$1@earth.execpc.com>,
rbean@earth.execpc.com (Ron Bean) wrote:

> I have to share various computers with other people, including
> both Mac's and PC's, so I just have to get used to the "standard"
> (ie, wrong) layout. But most Mac programs use the "command" key
> where the PC versions use the control key-- and they're in
> different places so I keep hitting the wrong one (grrrr). Do any
> Mac users remap the command key to be to the left of the A key?

Mac users generally get in the habit of using the right thumb for
the space key, the left one for the command key, and the right
pinky for the enter key.  I've never seen a command-key-moving-
extension but it would be no harder to write than it is for the PC.

Because the interface was designed from the ground-up to be used
with the mouse rather than the keyboard, most Mac users who don't
like the keyboard layout stick to the mouse.  The Human-Interface
guidelines are quite clear that you should never have to press a
succession of key combinations.  If it can't be done in one key-
press it belongs on a dialog and you should be using a pointer,
not key-strokes.  This pisses-off people who like keyboards so
there are all sorts of extension which define keystrokes to
arbitrary buttons and fields on arbitrary dialogs.

> There was a Sinclair laptop (Z8?) that had *rubber* keys. It was
> pretty wierd, but I think I could have gotten used to it (I
> decided not to buy the machine for other reasons).

I used a ZX-81 which had barely perceivable 'bubble' keys which was
tiring because they human hand isn't designed to control the
pressure on a finger to such great precision.  The 'rubber' keys
on the ZX-Spectrum increased the pressure needed so that you could
comfortably rest your fingers on the keyboard without pressing a
key by accident.  It was quite an improvement.  I used one for years
and bought LISP and FORTH interpreters for it.

Simon.
