> Ugh.  I'm a Windows user (unfortunate from time to time, but a fact of life
> none the less), and I make very frequent use of Ctrl, Alt, and that Windows
> key.  How else am I supposed to cut, copy, and paste (Ctrl-X, Ctrl-C,
> Ctrl-V), run menu commands (Alt-Something), or start my most commonly used
> applications (Windows-Something)?  I avoid the mouse as best I can.  You
> want to take these things away from me?  That's the making of a vile and
> evil man.

That was in response to a *graphics* man, Alberto C Moreira, who seems to
think his l^Hmousy way of working is best for everyone, but since I've
been accused of wanting to take away function, editing, scrolling, number
pad, and arrow keys and pointing devices, I'd like to make the point that
I do not. I just want Ctrl to be in the home row, and I want it to work
right, that is, I want it to be usable for *everything*. It is usable for
everything in my word processor, graphics program, Web browser, and mail
reader, and this in no way interferes with the ability to use a pointing
device or the redundant keys outside the main typing block. Other people
can have all the pointing devices and vendor-specific keys they want.
Replace the bottom-row Ctrl key with a new modifier key called Spcl or
some such, let that key work as Ctrl does now in GUIs, and return Ctrl to
its proper place and function for the many people who work primarily or
exclusively with text. Anything wrong with that?

> And for what it's worth, the Control key belongs on home row, CAPSLOCK
> belongs on the far side of Pluto, and Ctrl-H through Ctrl-L should be the
> four arrow keys.  (I hate taking my hands off home row to use the arrow
> keys...)

Ctrl-H/J/K/L is fine; I personally prefer Ctrl-E/S/D/X. Some of the other
arrangements I've seen in Unix, like Ctrl-P/N/B/F and Ctrl-Y/V/who-knows-
what, are the products of sick minds. What I want to know is why no
program uses something like Ctrl-U/J/K/N, a nice, tight, mnemonic diamond
that still leaves Ctrl-H for Backspace, Ctrl-I for Tab, and Ctrl-M for
Return.

Dan Strychalski
