DanteMatrix@Hell's.Gate wrote:
> I'm afraid you've been watching a wee bit too much court tv.
> Even if Intel is being deliberately malicious, it would not
> constitute an antitrust action.

The suggestion is that Intel has something approximating a monopoly
on i8088 compatible microprocessors.  (AMD and Cyrix compete in this
market, but the suggestion is that this competition is insufficient
to keep DEC from being hurt if DEC is denied access to Intel products.)
The suggestion is further that Intel will use it's position in the
i8088 microprocessor market to pressure DEC into allowing Intel to
use DEC's microprocessor patents without payment.  I believe that this
qualifies as a tying arrangement, which is an antitrust violation.  If
Intel is able to use it's market position to obtain microprocessor
technology free of charge, while Intel's competitors are forced to pay
for it, the result is anticompetitive.

Of course it has not yet been established that Intel infringed any of
DEC's patents.
				Kenneth Almquist



