handleym@apple.com (Maynard Handley) writes:

> People drag up VHS vs Beta all the time, but what real research has been
> done on the subject? For example
> * Beta was "better" than VHS. In what ways?

Beta had slightly better picture quality, while Video 2000 had better
sound quality.  VHS was in the middle on both accounts.  Beta had, IIRC,
shorter tapes (or faster moving), 2000 had the longest tapes, and they
were two sided.

> * Was VHS cheaper than Beta?

Probably.  If not initially, I'm sure the competition made it so.

> * Was VHS available from multiple vendors with a range of different
> options while Beta was single-sourced?

Initially, yes.

> * Did Beta cassettes jam up the machine more often than VHS?

No idea.

> or was it a content issue, that 
> * VHS cameras were available before/cheaper than Beta cameras, hence porn
> was all VHS?

Don't think so.  But VHS got a head start, and almost all rental movies
were in VHS format - and by then, the race was lost for competing
formats. 

In a way, it's very similar to the PC vs. Macintosh issue.  PCs are
multiple sourced (at least hardware wise), they have the largest
selection of software (but of course you don't need more than a few apps
- except for games which are more like video in that respect, I
suppose.  One could venture that the Mac became a dead end when the game
companies stopped shipping ports of games for it.)

> or was it purely a marketing issue, that
> * Beta commercials were really lame compared to VHS commercials?

Don't think so. :-)

~kzm
