> >
> 
> I agree completely.  In a different group, someone recently asked why
> people weren't switching over to Java faster.  One of the major reasons
> mentioned was speed.  To this, the person responded with the usual business

If you look closely at history you will see that about
15-10 years ago we all dropped assembler. We started using
C, C++, VB, etc.

No-one in their right mind uses assembler today for speed.
(I exclude all embedded systems and low-cost developers who
cannot just slap in a P-200 or whatever to get a better machine).

Today C is the base line, and more things are implemented in C++.
Most people dont care/realise the cost of the language they use.

Today I worry about performance but I wouldnt touch Java. Maybe in
10 years time when the machine on my desk is 100x faster I will
be using it. (I dont give a toss about games and all that other
garbage software out there).

Eventually it just seems that doing things the old hard way
puts you in a corner where you cannot compete.

This is what I am talking about - not that no-one needs to
worry when we have 100x CPU improvements. We do need 100x so
I can watch 20 TV channels all scaled to their own window on my
screen and recording them to disk at the same time, and listening
to my music coming in off the network at home :-) But these
things, although needed, will not affect the way we
use the CPU for s/w development.

