Aru Anidiot wrote:
>   What really makes me wonder is the question as to what DEC's plan
> is.  I mean, it's obvious that Intel would want to make things
> difficult for DEC once they filed suit, so what's the deal?
>
>   I'll admit that it sounds a bit like a big conspiracy theory, but
> I've heard DEC is really just trying to stop or stall Intel's
> processor production, and that it's not just a coincidence that DEC
> (a maker of a rival chip) made a deal with AMD (one of the few others
> making rival chips) right before the suit.  This is supposedly
> (according to this thinking) an attempt to seize some of the processor
> market.  If Intel can't make chips, who will the computer industry
> turn to?

Okay ... I'll admit that I've only been 'skimming' (there's far to much
to actually *read*) this topic, but there are a couple of factors that
people seem to have 'misplaced' in this discussion.

(Note: I'm assuming here that Intel actually has claimed patents on some
or all of what Dec is suing them for. It would *shock* me if that wasn't
the case.)

1) *Intel* stood to loose if Dec *didn't* sue. (see below).

2) If Dec *didn't* sue over these pattents, they would have lost them.
By my understanding of patent law, if you don't enforce your patents you
loose them.

3) If Dec lost their patents, a good portion of Pentium Technology would
have become *Public Domain* (anyone want to guess how that would hurt
intel? :)

   Why? As everyone knows, there is no such thing as an untold secret in
the Computer industry. Someone, somewhere would have found out that
Intel violated Dec's patents, and Dec didn't do anything about it. That
means that Dec's patents are invalid. All they have to do is prove
'prior art' on Intel's patents and boom. Intel becomes a *major* loser
int that particular portion of the chip wars.

  (BTW -- I readily admit that I have no idea just how much of the
Pentium is in Violation.)

4) As per above, Intel does *not* want to declare Dec's Patents invalid.
If they do, they loose their *own* patents. They're only two real
choices is to settle out of court (ie. - liscence the technology), or to
prove that the opposing patents are 'sufficiently different'.
Apparently, though, by the 'experts' on CPU technologies I've heard,
that ain't gonna happen.

5) Outcomes: 
             1) Intel proves that they are not violating patents, and
all is hunky dory. (Like Dec would have kept the 'favored customer
status' anyway! They're moving in against Intel's Top line!)
             2)(settle out of court) Dec gets Intel to license the
technology and pay 'damages'. Dec then get's to liscence the technology
to Intel's Competitors, and wins a 'piece of the action'. They can also
stipulate in the settlement that Intel has to keep suplying them or
whatever.
             3) Intel gets a 'brain cramp', proceeds with the suit, and
looses. All the notes in two apply, with added fines and court costs.



Intel, contrary to what some seem to believe, is not stupid. They will,
IMHO, settle out of court unless they are *real* sure that the patents
are not being violated.

Course I suppose it's *possible* that we'll get some more 'public
domain' technology out of this, but I wouldn't hold my breath.

John Price
CHI Systems



