The premise of your argument seems to be that DEC needs the Deschutes
yellow book in order to be an effective competitor in the computer
market place.  This premise is endemic of Intel's relentless marketing
campaign for the past many years.  Over the years, we have all been
convinced by Intel that we need ever faster and more powerful microprocessors.
Intel has a vested interest in ensuring that their microprocessor is
*ALWAYS* the bottleneck in the computer system.  As such, you will need
ever faster and more powerful microprocessors.  (Historically speaking,
INtel's past actions, eg NSP and MMX initiatives, support this viewpoint.)

Have you considered that DEC's business model may not buy in to this plan?
(I don't know if it does, or doesn't.)  As such, they might not necessarily
need the Deschutes yellow book.  Furthermore, have you ever considered
the bigger question:  do you need a Pentium II or Deschutes microprocessor?

