Steve Kahler wrote:

> 
> I build servers and I can tell you that if DEC allowed anyone to make
> motherboards for the Alpha (as Intel does with the Pentium) they would
> cost about $250 instead of $4000 as Alpha MB's do now.

They actually DO!!

You can even download the schematics and gerber files for the PC164
board.
Also the source code for the SROM plus some of their SDK stuff, are
available
on their web-site:


http://ftp.digital.com/pub/Digital/info/semiconductor/alpha/dsc-software-alpha.html

You can also get the SDK and PC164-MB-design-files from your local
DEC-Semiconductor-Distributor, on a CDROM for about $100.

I myself did a couple of designes with Alpha, and although my Alpha
boards
were designed for Industrial purposes, I used DEC's 21171/72 chipset,
and thereby saved
a lot of developing time in copying the memory and cache part from the
schematics.

The SDK containes a lot of nice tools for developing purposes,
especially the 
SROM-Debug-Monitor, which allows you to run and monitor the processor
from a
PC-COM port via the Alpha SROM-Port. This way you can start and test
your MB-design
from the processor.
If your design is excactly like the PC164, their is also a larger
Debug-monitor,
that will allow you to run programs from a floppy, and Monitor all via
EtherNet,
on a PC.
If you choose, not to design your board like the PC164, source code for
the Debuggers
are contained on the SDK, and you can easyly rewrite whatsever is
nessersary.

If you do not want to invest lots of money in OS's and Compiler's, you
can buy
Linux from REDHAT for about $100, which besides a nice OS, contains all
sorts
of compilers and utilities.

While I was in the early stages of my Alpha-Board-designs, I had great
support from
DEC in Reading(UK), and I was never charged a cent for that support:')

A PC164 board w.o. processor, today cost's between $800 and $900, the
Alpha 21164
processor is priced from $650-$1500 depending on the speed.
Now given that the rest of the system for making a NT-box, is done with
standard
PC component's, you should be able to put together an Alpha NT Box for
the sum
of $3500(and this is with DEC's own MB).
The fact that this Alpha-NT box will perform 2-3 times a simular
equipped PPro200,
makes the price quite reasonable.


Samsung and Mitshubitsi will from now on start to make Alpha
processores, and VLSI
has designed an altenative chipset, and it shall be very interesting to
follow the
prices when these two giant's make's their entry into the PC market with
Alpha-boxes.
 

From my point of view, DEC has done a great job with the Alpha
processor, they
give a very nice support on their product's, but they simply forgot how
to 
advertice it.

regards

	Anders Blicher Petersen



