Steve Kahler <sdkahler@webrowse.com> writes:

>I wonder if one of those documents that Intel wants back shows a
>picture of an INTEL processor on a cheap Taiwan made motherboard ?

>If so then I would suggest that DEC study this document carefully 
>before returning it to Intel.   They might learn something about selling
>mass quantities of chips.

I, too, would love to see cheap Alpha systems, but I'm not sure
the above is fair.

Historically, Digital has not +tried+ to sell Alpha to
the mass market, so it's hardly surprising they've failed.

And they're currently working to bring Alpha more into
the mainstream.  At $495, a 533MHz 21164PC chip costs less
than a 200MHz PPro or a 233MHz PII, though it does have the
big disadvantage of twice the SPECfp95 performance.

As for system costs, back in November, DEC ran the
following estimates for the PII and 21164PC systems:

  Subsystem                           Klamath-233 21164PC
  Microprocessor/Chipset/Cache RAM        475     425
  Motherboard                             143     239
  32MB EDO/S DRAM                         160     160
  Graphics64-bit/2MB      SG               55      55
  2.4GB Hard-disk Drive                   160     160
  Floppy, 8x CD-ROM Drive                  61      61
  Case/Power Supply                        40      80
  17inch  Monitor                         360     360
  Keyboard & Mouse                         17      17
  Windows NT,Software,Packaging           203     203
  Total Cost                             1674    1760
  Gross Margin (30%)                      502     528
  Total System Price                     2176    2288
  Estimated SPECint95/SPECfp95            9/7   14/17

The 21164PC looks to be very competitive. Granted, at this
price it runs NT, not Digital UNIX.  But how cheap are
comparable Sun and HP PA-RISC systems running the UNIX
of those competitors?

	-doug



