zalman@netcom.com (Zalman Stern) writes:

> Joe Buck (jbuck@synopsys.com) wrote:
> [BetaMax vs. VHS.]
> 
> Wasn't VHS originally developed by RCA?

This is getting a bit far afield, but VHS was started by Sony who
dropped it at a very preliminary level and developed the Beta format.
JVC (Japan Victor) bought the rights to the early Sony work (not yet
called VHS) and developed it into the original VHS format.

Most patents for VHS are owned by JVC and all VHS machines are
produced under license from JVC.

RCA was one of the original proponents of VHS and sold some of the
first VHS units in the US.  Later, when Sony introduced the longer-play
Beta II format, RCA engineers developed a longer play version of VHS
which they called LP.

This was done without the approval of JVC who was busy working on the
even longer play EP version. (RCA and its allies called this speed
SLP, just to further confusion.) JVC has fought the LP speed for years
and the license agreement for SVHS prohibits the production of SVHS
units which can record at the intermediate speed.


Oddly, one of the strong supporters of the LP speed is Matsushita
(Panasonic) who manufactured all of the early RCA VCRs. This is odd
because Matsushita owns the controlling interest of the much smaller
JVC.

Also, for non-US/Canadian reader, these speeds are used ONLY in the US
and Canada. European speeds are universal as the RCA work was not
applicable to machines running at 625 line frames.

Now, aren't you sorry you asked?
