I think you mean you don't put many control codes into the material you
work on. Neither do I, these days, and that's not what I'm talking about.
Since control codes are meant to control things and not to print, Ctrl-
key combinations are an excellent way of doing everything you have to do
besides typing and drawing (and they're good for doing simple vector
graphics, too). Moving the cursor, scrolling the screen, opening menus,
navigating through those menus -- stuff like that. My job (and that of a
fair number of other people) is to crank out text. Keeping my hands in
typing position all the time seems like a good habit to me. Computer
keyboards and software were once designed to make that possible. Now most
of them are designed to make it impossible.

>                                                 I bet you use your
> backspace key a lot more, and that's just as far from your home keys.

I'm glad you mentioned that. I never, never, NEVER use my backspace key.
Ctrl-H is backspace in ASCII, ECMA-6, and all ISO encoding methods,
including Unicode. It is backspace on the CP/M, MS/PC/DR DOS, and Unix
command lines, in all CP/M and early DOS apps, in most Unix apps, and
even in a few situations in W-----s (probably the few parts the
programmers had to test themselves before the stuff went out the door).

I also hit Ctrl-[ more often than Esc, Ctrl-I at least as often as Tab,
and Ctrl-M about as often as Return/Enter. Each does the same thing as
the corresponding dedicated key, according to the ANSI/ECMA/ISO standards
that make this text readable to you.

I do a LOT of backspacing. Using a long-standard home-row keystroke for
backspace seems like a good habit to me. These days, though, I have to
jump through hoops to be able to do that: run one key-swapping TSR for
this system, another for that system, modify this app's key bindings,
that app's key map.... I personally don't mind fiddling a bit -- that's
how I learned everything I know about computers -- but overall, the
situation is pretty galling.

Dan Strychalski
dski at cameonet cameo com tw (not "twx" as in the header)



