In article <5mfmpv$26e$1@wagner.spc.videotron.ca>, jfmezei <"jfmezei"@videotron.ca.[no.spam]> writes:
|>I too have inherited from an old VAXstation 3100 with the 54meg RZ disk.
|>(I think it is a 3100model 30)
|>
|>I have 2 questions:
|>
|>1-Considering that this box was built during the height of DEC's
|>  proprietary era (the external SCSI connector being a good example),
|>  is the SCSI controller such that it will only accept Digital SCSI 
|>  drives or would it work with newer drives that are not DEC ? 
|>  Is that a restriction of the hardware or the operating system version
|>?

There are at least three different sorts of SCSI connectors that I have seen
on Digital kit and elsewhere over the years. None of them are Digital 
proprietary.

Back in those days, the SCSI spec was very loose and different drives 
implemented different things. You can almost certainly plug in any SCSI
drive and it will work at the hardware level. The system software may or may
not function correctly with that drive however. Later software (e.g. recent
revisions of OpenVMS) tries harder to allow for these quirks.

|>
|>2-I would rather have the box as a VAXserver than a VAXstation. This
|>  is done by plugging a terminal in the printer port instead of 
|>  plugging a keyboard in the keyvboard port. Right ? 

You also need to change the setting of S3 (the tiny little switch at the
back and to the right - there may or may not be a cut-out for it in the
case, depending on how early a Model 30 you have).


