In news:338cc331.9660593@news.demon.co.uk "Sam" (sam@greenaum.demon.co.uk)
wrote --

> I didn't complain when my Atari XE had a different delete key from my
> Spectrum, I just got used to it. It's not "wrong" it's just different.

Going from manual typewriters to electric typewriters to IBM mag-cards to
line-display electronic typewriters to Apple IIs to Esprit data terminals
and finally to IBM-type micros with backslash/bar, acute/tilde, and Esc
every which where, I found some things mildly annoying (like IBM's
refusal to use once-standard ASCII-based number/symbol pairings), some
things downright amusing (like having backslash/bar down where the only
efficient way to hit it is with the knuckle of my right pinky), and some
things pleasantly surprising (like how well the tiny space bar on my
laptop worked out). I went from working shift keys to three years of
hitting Esc to get caps (that was on the Apple II; Esc was where IBM puts
Tab) and then back to working shift keys. No problem. Acute/tilde and
backslash/bar are in different places on my office and home machines. No
problem. I occasionally have to type Chinese using a non-Latin phonetic
alphabet, and I'm amazed at how quickly I start remembering where each
symbol is. I figure if the letter keys were all scrambled around I'd do
fine, as long as the most important keys were in the middle three rows.

And on a computer, Ctrl is the most important key of all.

