In article <borsomEAwBIG.MKr@netcom.com>,
Douglas Borsom <borsom@netcom.com> wrote:
>The only interesting thing about this is that when
>Intel requested return of their putative confidential
>info, Digital apparently told them to bugger off.
>

DEC was very wise in refusing to return Intel's yellow books.
Intel seems to have had a little of a bad history with renegade
employees who purport to be acting on the company's behalf.
There was the case of the Intel employee (Melanie Yunk) who
contacted my employer (TI) when I started my Intel Secrets
web site.  She didn't like what I was doing, and alleged to
my employer that I had somehow violated INtel's computer network 
security.  She encouraged my employer to "take the appropriate
action" against me.  Then there was the case of two Intel
executives who recently threatened to withdraw advertising funds
from two German magazines, because they supported the web site
known as Tom's Hardware Guide.  There was such a public outcry
because of this situation that Intel was forced to take out
a full-page ad in the New York Times in which they confessed
their sins, admitted it was wrong, and apologized.  In both of
these situations, renegade Intel employees were claiming to
be acting on behalf of Intel corporation, but apparently were
not authorized to do such things.

So, I can certainly understand why DEC would refuse to give back
Intel's yellow books until they had personally heard from Intel's
Vice President, and Chief Counsel, Thomas Dunlap Jr.  Given the
history of overzealous renegade Intel employees, can you blame DEC
for taking this stance?  Wouldn't you want to make sure that
the request to return the documents was an authentic request from
Intel Corporation?  How else could DEC do this, without hearing
from Dunlap himself?  Given Intel's past history, I believe that
DEC acted wisely and prudently.

Intel, for their part, did what was expected of them:  they filed
a tit-for-tat lawsuit against DEC.  Amuzingly, Intel's response
was more like firing a water pistol to DEC's firing of ICBMs.



