From: everhart@arisia.gce.com (Glenn C. Everhart)
Subject: Re: VAXstation 3100
Date: 1997/05/29
Message-ID: <5mjv32$18t$1@mrnews.mro.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 244741207
Distribution: world
References: <3384AE16.740E@mail.idt.net> <5m4jd3$f8i@usenet.pa.dec.com> <338A6F84.7DB@home.com> <5mfmpv$26e$1@wagner.spc.videotron.ca>
Organization: DEC
Reply-To: Everhart@Arisia.GCE.Com
Newsgroups: comp.os.vms,comp.sys.dec



A VS3100 has a connector that is uncommon but actually is not only a DEC
one. The machine will work with a variety of non-DEC SCSI devices.

None of the VMS drivers restricts operation to DEC devices,at least in
SCSI-land. (I don't recall any such restriction in un-obvious places
anywhere else but have not investigated.) Any SCSI disk may work, but
you must remember that there are a lot of weird things some SCSI devices
do that aren't quite standard, and there may be some SCSI responses that
the VMS drivers don't anticipate. What is tested is basically DEC stuff.

Remember that the bulk of the disk market is in the PC space, and in
that space, it is common not to need to ever disconnect the SCSI bus
during a transfer. VMS always expects disconnect. In the PC space it
is common (the norm, really) not to have disks doing DMA. The VMS norm
is to do DMA. VMS also tends to be very careful (I won't say paranoid)
about avoiding data corruption or loss. This has caused grief with
some disks that don't deal properly with this. (I've seen the SCSI bus
traces to prove this!)

Also there are occasional bugs which cause problems with particular
devices. We get patches out to alleviate those problems. 

BTW, the vaxserver/vaxstation decision depends on whether a keyboard
is plugged in. The console choice can be a terminal regardless of this...
even a remote terminal (e.g., via LAT server). If you don't want the
big monitor on a vs3100 but still are using it the same, just leave
the keyboard in but out of the way and use a terminal. The bulk of the
monitor does not have to be there, and keyboards are pretty thin (and even
inexpensive). As for licensing more users, that is something I can't speak to.

Back to SCSI. Bottom line is that the Digital drivers do not now, nor have
ever (far as I know) limited use to Digital branded disks/tapes/etc.
They do tend to know more about Digital qualified stuff, and the Digital
qual process weeds out a lot of broken behavior. However there is no attempt
to prevent 3rd party stuff from working, and in fact rather a lot of work
goes into trying to allow it. But we can only test what we see, which is
basically all Digital qual'd stuff. There are a LOT of different peripherals
and systems to test on, so it's well and truly into a combinatorial explosion
as it is doing all these tests and trying to respond to complaints from
the field. A few folks can only do so much...

Glenn Everhart



