I think that DEC's lawsuit is really stupid !!!!!

The REAL PROBLEM with DEC's low sales of the Alpha Chip is their
marketing practices.

They only allow certain people to sell motherboards with the Alpha 
chip on it.    This results in motherboards that cost $4,000 which 
is totally ridiculous and insane !

I call this the "Apple control-freak syndrome".   They want total
control over the Alpha chip and who sells it so they can satisfy
their control-freak tendencies.   But as witnessed by the Apple fiasco 
this is a disastrous marketing strategy.

They should just sell the Alpha chip to ANYONE with green money and let
them do as they please with it.   If they want to stick it in their
garden and spray it with water let them do it !!!

That's what Intel does.   Intel doesn't go around bullying people
who are making Pentium motherboards and telling them they can't.  They
allow ANYONE to make motherboards with their chip on it.

The end result is that a DUAL-PROCESSOR Pentium MB costs around $250
street price.

I'd love to build servers with Alpha chips but the motherboards just
cost too much.   The price-performance is not there for the Alpha
when you take into account what the motherboards are being sold for.

I've done engineering work in the electronics business and I know that
circuit boards can be made dirt cheap when stamped out in volumes.

I've also seen the DEC announcements about the price reductions (and
prices) of their Alpha chips so I know hopw much vendors are marking
these motherboards up.  

Some quick calculations show me that they are raking in huge profits 
on those printed circuit boards.  They're probably making far more money 
on the Alpha than DEC is.

If DEC wants to sell lots of Alpha Chips they don't need to lower the
chip price anymore.   At the current price level with a REASONABLY
priced circuit board (probably made in Taiwan) they could blow Intel
out of the water.

They just need to cut out all of the licensing nonsense with the Alpha
and just sell it ANYONE who has green money.

When I but Intel motherboards and chips the only qualification I know of
is that you're money is good.   There's no stupid licensing involved.

This is the way capitalism should work:   
	You have a product, I have money, you sell it to me, PERIOD. 

	I don't need to prove I'm a loyal American or ofr a certain race,
creed, color to but your product ---- the only thing you should be
concerned about is the bottom line ---- money !

If DEC followed my simple marketing strategy they'd be selling tons of
these Alpha chips.   If the customer has the money you sell it to him or
her --- that's about as simple as it can get.

You don't need to care how the customer is using your product --- Intel
doesn't.  The only thing they care about is counting the money.

Not everyone wants to buy a Cadillac.  If someone wants to make Fords
using your product why should you care ?  IBM and Intel don't.

People who want to but a Cadillac could still go to DEC or one of the
"approved Alpha vendors" and buy it.

Let us Ford buyers (who vastly out number the Caddy folks) have
something to buy.

Computers become obsolete so rapidly that it doesn't make any sense 
to buy an expensive model.   I always look for the cheapest.

I think that you'll find there are a lot of Ford buyers who would jump
at the chance of getting the performance of an Alpha chip at a
reasonable price.

		Sincerely,

		Steve Kahler
		We Browse, Inc.

PS.  I've been in the PC business for 17 years and the secret of the
whole thing is to put lots of cheap models out there to get market
share.   The cheap chinese imports are what made the PC what it is.
	The Alpha is a fine product and has vastly better performance than
anything Intel has.
	Just let it loose on the market (grey market, garage PC's, chinese
PC's, etc.) and see what happens.



