> Read the AP Wires, or even look at CNET News.  Hell,
> just ready any computer publication, and you can
> search for that 5% market share figure.  Unless it's
> a Mac publication of course.  Since they suffer from
> the same mental block as their OS.  They might actually
> report a 90% Mac market share.

I'll tell you what. You show me a single Mac magazine which claims a 90%
market share and I'll eat my hat. 

In fact, I doubt if you'll even find a Mac publication which publishes
incorrect market share information. Please prove your allegations since
what you just stated amounts to libel.

As for 5% market share, when are you going to learn that market share is a
function of what you're measuring. 

If  you're measuring sales in the most recent quarter, it's not too far
off--by Dataquest's method. Someone else's method might give a slightly
different result since there's no way to provide an exact figure.

If you're talking about installed base, the Mac has a much higher installed
base than that. 

If you're talking about software sales, the Mac is typically 15-20% market
share.

If you're talking about professional graphics, the Mac is about 80% market
share.

If you're talking about web design, the Mac is around 60% market share.

And so on.

