John  Bayko <bayko@borealis.cs.uregina.ca> wrote
> 
>     Are you saying you wouldn't want to expand your customer base by
> 10% (or possibly a more, since that figure is a little exaggerated in
> some ways) for (almost) free?
> 

Possibly, but it's not free.  It's not even almost free.

> 
>     There's nothing stopping a developer from coding speed critical
> parts of a Java application to run natively - heck, that's how the AWT
> interfaces with the native OS GUI (ack, three TLAs in a single
> sentence!). In face, you said you're doing that now - mixing assembly
> with C++.
>

I write graphics engines (libraries) to be used in applications, not
(usually) the applications themselves.  It seems kind of screwy to write
the fast code in C++/Asm, and then put a Java wrapper around it. 
Especially since this is only "portable" to as many platforms as I have
compiled the native code.  Now maybe the applications and tools people at
my company should do this...  but there is still the argument that you only
need the tools for the platforms where the library runs.

>
>     Remember, Merced/IA-64 is coming. It may be big, or it may be a
> bust. Either way, the only way to win no matter which happens is to
> pay attention to portability in a big way.
> 

In the two years I've been doing this, I've rewritten (from scratch) the
whole library several times.  Two or three times because I've decided on a
better design, and now because I want to make use of the MMX stuff for what
it's worth.  When the P7 comes out, I'll probably rewrite it again to take
advantage of whatever I can on that platform.

As long as there is one company holding a significant majority of the
market, the company I work for and I will probably continue to cater to
that majority.


I did see something involving Java I thought was pretty cool though.  A
teacher at the UofA (one of my Profs from a couple years ago) is planning
to write a set of high speed compilers for Java that compile native to
whatever Unix platforms the user is running on.  I think the idea is to
send the source code instead of the byte code, and as long as the compiler
can process the stream faster than the network connection can supply it,
the user would never need to know.  This is a pretty cool idea as you get
the best of both worlds - portability and native compilation for a fast
executable.  This only works for apps where the source code is public of
course.






