> (I recall Andy posting that the x86 wasn't all that bad actually and there
> was no good reason to abandon it except perhaps for the difficulty of
> extending the ISA to 64-bits. I'd have expected a much more vigorous
> defense of "Intel's" architectural prowess here. Perhaps the shock and
> horror of being libeled by a wussy RISC toting competitor's CEO has
> temporarily lessened his resolve :-))

Awhhh, for Christ's Sake, Zalman, I was designing RISC microprocessors
(on paper, as a class project) before the term RISC was coined.

And, as I have said many times in this group, I still think
that my name - RAMM, Reduced Addressing Mode Machine - is more
accurate than RISC.

But even though I was an early RISC proponent, I still say that
the x86 isn't that bad. It's certainly not as bad as the VAX or
68K.

I was interested in RISC, in the early 80s, because I thought then
that it might be necessary to use a RISC instruction set in order
to make out-of-order CPU design possible.  It turns out that I was
wrong - that the overhead of handling so-called CISC instructions,
as long as they aren't too complicated, is not so big.

The IBM 360 would have been one of the easiest CISCs to apply OOO
to. The x86 only a little harder. The 68K and VAX quite a bit harder.

Yeah, RISC has an advantage: maybe 15%. IMHO not enough to be worth
a change.



The shock and horror of being slandered by Digital is due mainly to the
fact that Digital was and is so far off the curve. Digital was the
leading advocate of in-order microarchitecture, Intel the leading
advocate
of out-of-order design. (That was then, now everyone is on the 
out-of-order bandwagon.)



