In article <5mfuid$53a$1@gryphon.phoenix.net>, mike_st@iah.com (Mike 
Stubblefield) wrote:
[snip]

>
> SCS NODE: WOTAN                                               VAX/VMS V5.5-2
>
VMS 5.5-2

[snip]
>       MSLG$W_EVENT        0069
>                                       HOST BUFFER ACCESS ERROR
>                                       NON-EXISTENT MEMORY ERROR
Sounds like the KDA50 can't access the user's I/O buffer.
This could be a KDA50 problem, a bus problem (e.g. a broken
address line), a memory problem, a QBUS map problem, or
a memory management problem.  *OR* it could be a software
problem.   Have you tried a more recent version of VMS, or
looked for any DUDRIVER patches for V5.5-2, or SYS patches
affecting your CPU?  You have lots of QBUS DMA devices;
maybe you are running out of QBUS mapping registers, and
VMS isn't handling it correctly.

I would also try removing non-essential devices and see if
that helps.  I would take out everything that you don't need for
booting (Ethernet,  PUB, PUC, PUD disk controllers, any tape
controller.)   The DZQ is probably irrelevent, since it isn't DMA.

The testing you have done probably eliminates QBUS map
or memory physical problems, and since everything works
with a MVII, there's a good chance it isn't a backplane or
broken KDA50 (or other broken device interfering with the
KDA50.  (I'm assuming the MVII had more than 4 MB of
memory, and thus was exercising the QBUS map;  I'm
not sure but I/O might be direct rather than mapped on
systems with less than 4 MB.)

This basically leaves software.  Depopulating the bus
might work around a software problem (and thus
confirm that the KDA50 is okay), if the problem is
caused by too many DMA I/O devices.   (5 disk
controllers is *many*.)  Software problems usually
happen under extreme conditions, not under normal
conditions, and the only out-of-the-ordinary thing I
see here is the number of controllers.  Also, there
are separate CPU-support files for each CPU
(the SYSLOA*.EXE files), and there is a different
one of these for MVII's and VAX 3900's.  These
files hanlde CPU-dependent things, like QBUS
mapping, and so there could easily be a bug in this
that only affects 3900's and not MVII's.

HTH
John Santos


>-mike Stubblefield - 



