Actually, the Discover article was pretty disappointing.  There was an
article in Reason that actually did a little research on the subject.
The main study in favor of the Dvorak keyboard was conducted for the
Navy by Dvorak himself (at the time holding the patent on the Dvorak
keyboard).  There were various problems with the study.

A later (more modern, more scientific) HF study conducted by IBM gave
IMHO the expected result:

take a QWERTY typist, give them training (in DVORAK), they end up
typing faster.

But they also took QWERTY typists, gave them training (an equal
amount, in QWERTY), who also ended up faster.

[...]

>Entered on my Dell 104-key QWERTY keyboard, with the '\' key between
>'Enter' and 'Backspace' (Yay) and 'Ctrl' in the lower-left corner
>(Boo).

Entered on one of those MS keyboards.  My wrists don't move at all, so
I'm skeptical that QWERTY necessarily implies injury.





