Regarding the backslash/bar key, Stuart Lynne (sl@whiskey.poste.com)
wrote in news:5mgtrf$rat@whiskey.poste.com --

> No it's **supposed** to be above the enter and below the backspace
> key. That's the true IBM way... :-)

Not between Z and left Shift as on the original 5150?

It's next to Backspace on my desktop boards and below it on my laptop. I
find I prefer the laptop's arrangement. It means a much smaller Return/
Enter key, but that's OK. IBM's cancerous Enter keys make no sense to me.

> I find that the easiest to touch type with (you certainly can't
> go wrong with *real* IBM/Lexmark keyboards for touch typing and
> they last forever or at least until you spill coffee on them).

Keep in mind that much more than touch typing is involved. In combination
with the Ctrl key, 32 of the character keys generate command codes common
to every computer on the face of the earth. Some people might prefer
dedicated function, editing, and arrow keys, some might prefer a pointing
device, but Ctrl-key combinations are a resource that can be valuable to
many -- and almost all commercial software for x86-based machines ignores
them or uses them for trivial or undocumented purposes. They were
*completely* disabled in all the apps bundled with Windows 1.0, and
shortly after that IBM moved Ctrl out of the main key block.

What gives?

