one or more of Intel's thousand-odd patents; the thing will be settled
as soon as the lawyers get a good feel for the relative strength of the
two sides' cases.  DEC may get access to some Intel patents or some
other concession.  As usual, the lawyers will come out with the most.

>The court could possibly instruct Intel to remove the offending
>technologies as quickly as possible, while paying tolerable fines and
>penalties to Digital; but they can't ask Intel to simply stop shipping
>Pentiums.  The damage done would be to you and me.

This doesn't matter; the court doesn't get to care about you and me.
It must rule on the law, and the law says patent holders have a monopoly.
Don't worry, though; court proceedings of this type are so slow that
any ruling might come five years from now.

In my industry (electronic design automation), it seems that at any time
several major vendors are suing each other.  In the FPGA/CPLD industry
it's even worse.  Life goes on.

