Hello,

Reinier Mostert <Reinier.Mostert@afa.co.za> je napisal:
> On a related note - I have a Data General 20" screen circa 1987, model no
> 6335-2 (I think (I can make sure if necessary)), that has three BNC
> connectors on the back, R, G and B.  I have a 15 pin VGA to BNC converter
> cable, but so far I can't get the screen to work.  The screen is in a
> working condition, but with my cable there is nothing on the display.  I
> assume this is because the screen is fixed frequency, and I need the right
> resolution and refresh rate to make it go.

There are two things to keep in mind when dealing with older high-end
monitors:

- fixed frequency. If you have the exact specifications for the monitor
  (horizontal frequency, vertical frequency), then you can tweak
  modelines for Xserver so that it would work.

- most of them don't have separate sync BNC connectors. Therefore they
  receive sync signals mixed with gree signals (sync-on-green).
  Not all of (low-cost) graphic adapters are able to do that. Check
  the documentation of your X server and of your card if it works.
  Actually, the documentation of X server is the place to look at.
  I've had a Matrox Millenium card and the documentation doesn't
  mention sync-on-green capability, while XFree86 server for that
  card supports it.

Regards,


