In comp.benchmarks David Coote <dccoote@werple.mira.net.au> wrote:
> If you look at the clock speed and super-scalar capabilities of a 600MHz Alpha 
> 21164 you've theoretically got a chip capable of 2.4 GIPS. But unless you had 
> an application that fitted - text and data - in L1 cache (thus having no 
> requirements to access other memory in the hierarchy) and contained code that 
> could run 4-way super-scalar each clock cycle, you won't see this performance. 

Yeah 2.4 GIPS peak is a useless measure of performance for most things...

> Real applications have branches, clobber the pipeline, don't have neat 
> arrangements of 2 single cycle integer and 2 single cycle fp instructions 
> ready for dispatching each clock cycle, won't fit in cache - whether L1, L2 or 
> L3 - need to access main memory, saturate the available memory bandwidth, 
> access (shudder) disk etc etc.

Right, I suggest checking out www.specbench.org, the specfp95 suite
uses main memory quite a bit, and use "real" codes not synthetic
cache friendly benchmarks.  (I.e. bytemarks).

Here's a summary of the specfp95 benchmarks (from the www site):
Fluid Dynamics / Geometric Translation      Generation of a two-dimensional
                                            boundary-fitted coordinate system
                                            around general geometric domains.

Weather Prediction      Solves shallow water equations using finite
                        difference approximations. (The only single
                        precision benchmark in CFP95.)

Quantum Physics         Masses of elementary particles are
                        computed in the Quark-Gluon theory.

Astrophysics            Hydrodynamical Navier Stokes equations
                        are used to compute galactic jets.

Electromagnetism        Calculation of a 3D potential field.

Fluid Dynamics/Math     Solves matrix system with pivoting.

Simulation              Simulates turbulence in a cubic area.

Weather Predication     Calculates statistics on temperature and
                                            pollutants in a grid.

Chemistry               Performs multi-electron derivatives.

Electromagnetics        Solve's Maxwell's equations on a cartesian mesh.
                                                                             


>Most of the "reputable" benchmarks I've seen for the Alpha predict performance 
>based on small applications that don't have a lot of relevance to large 
>applications with a lot of IO written in languages such as C, Pascal or Ada. 

Not seen any pascal or ada benchmarks.  If by I/O you meant disk/network
I/O specint95/fp95 won't help.  They do use main memory quite a bit.




