Scott Gilbert wrote:

> 
> >
> > It is obvious that Java will fail and not be adopted due to the lack of
> > a native compiler.  Remember the utter failure and disappearance from
> > the market of Microsoft's Visual Basic for just that reason?
> >
>

This is not strictly true, since many Java Virtual Machines now include
what are known as 'Just In Time' (JIT) compilers. These take the Java
Byte Code (once it's gone through all the mandatory security checks) and
compile it into native machine code. Since the compilation is done
within the JVM, the platform-specific part of the environment, you don't
lose any of the Operating System independence of Java but do gain (some
of) the execution speed of native code. (I say 'some of' because all of
the time that an application spends within the JVM, which is often
significant is not accelerated any further). The extra time taken to
compile the code is usually negligable (and can probably be overlapped
with the download and verification processes, anyway).

And no, I'm not going to pretend that Visual Basic has sunk without
trace (yet!). 

Ian.


All the usual disclaimers (about not speaking on behalf of Sun
Microsystems in any official capacity) apply.



