In article <3389FC4B.77DF@pgec.odu.edu>, 
Jason Witty <jwitty@pgec.odu.edu> writes:

> I just got a VaxServer 3300, with no
> additional hardware (terminals, keyboards, etc.) and have no idea how to
> interface it.  Pulling off the front cover reveals a plug-in card which
> looks like some sort of I/O card with the markings MS650-AA, M7621-A,
> KA640-BA, and M7621-BA near the top of the card.  

The first (rightmost) card in the backplane should be a M7624-xx,
where "xx" is one of several versions.   This is the CPU card, and
is also known as a KA640-xx.   The Technical Manual is DEC part number
EK-KA640-TM.   There are three ribbon cable connectors on the board,
one by itself and two stacked.   The one by itself connects to the
front panel controls and connectors.

Of the stacked pair, the one closest to the board is the interconnect
to the memory board(s) (see below) in the next slot.   The one
furthest from the board is for the on-board DSSI disk controller.
DSSI is similar to SCSI at the physical/electrical level, but is
vastly different at higher levels.

As you have already deduced, it has an on-board Ethernet controller
with both AUI and 10Base2 connectors.   As you guessed, the modular
connector with the offset key (called MMJ -- Modified Modular Jack)
is for the terminal.   The speed is set by the rotary switch at the
top of the PC board inside the front panel (called the "cabinet kit").

The second (and possibly third) board in the backplane should be
a memory board.   The Module number is M7621-xx and its name is
MS650-xx.

> If so, how can I obtain a terminal for it?  

Any serial terminal (or PC terminal emulator) will work with this
VAX, but I cannot remember the pinouts for the MMJ.   Perhaps
someone else here has them handy?

This VAX will run either VMS or DEC Unix (or whatever they are
calling it this week).   DEC and DECUS have just announced a
free hobbyist license for VMS, with a cheap (~$25) distribution
kit on CDROM.   If you can find a VCB02 graphics option (M7168
and M7169) it makes a nice VMS workstation.

Further information on VMS can be found in comp.os.vms.
I am happy to answer any questions you might have about the 3300.

