In article <33851214.13A5@mosquitonet.com>, Joe Kennedy <nospam-jkennedy@mosquitonet.com> writes:
> Is there a way possible to dismount the system disk on a uvax system
> (VMS 4.7, one RA60 drive, 8MB RAM), then swap packs and mount the new
> pack?

Depends on what your goal is and whether you'll tolerate down time.

Given the above configuration, I can't see any way to do it on-line.

If you could convert your system disk into a shadow set and you had
a way to temporarily access another RA60 sized shadow member then you
could swap shadow members and wind up with a different pack underneath
your single member shadow set.  But you've only got the one drive and
you don't have enough RAM for a RA60 sized virtual disk in memory.

It's barely conceivable that you could do a BACKUP /PHYSICAL to tape
and then a BACKUP /PHYSICAL restore to a different unit number on the
same drive (with major shuffling of unit plugs and disk packs as
you give mount verification some real work to do) and then finally put
the copied disk in place with the original unit plug, trusting that mount
verification wouldn't notice the substitution.  God save you from the
writes the file system thought it had committed to the system disk while
while you were doing all this.  *shudder*

The obvious move and the way I'd handle this would be to:

	1.  Make sure you have a stand-alone backup kit somewhere.  If
	    not, build one on your current system disk.

	2.  Shut down the system

	3.  Reboot into stand-alone backup

	4.  Backup the current system disk to tape

	5.  Swap packs

	6.  Restore from tape to the new system disk

	7.  Reboot from the new pack.

If you want to save time, you could BACKUP /IMAGE to tape before the
shutdown.  Or you could use standalone BACKUP /PHYSICAL.

	John Briggs			vaxs09@alpha.vitro.com



