Rumor has it that Intel/HP Merced processors will offer anywhere from 5-15
times the performance of today's x86 and PA-RISC cpu's. That remains to be
seen, but based on the various VLIW reports and exising VLIW designs, it is
possible for them to build a 700-1400Mhz cpu capable of issuing a large
number of instructions per cycle. Assuming that the cpu can emulate x86 and
PA-RISC instructions at a rate of 1 per cycle, the chip would still rival a
700-1400Mhz Pentium! Native VLIW code would run much faster.

VLIW has some serious issues though. It is very compiler dependent to the
point that code could have to be recompiled for every new cpu generation or
even extreme differences in clock speed. The cpu will also need an very
wide, very fast memory subsystem to keep fed. At the same time the chip
could be very cheap to mass produce. 

I have a feeling that HP can pull it off. That level of performance
improvement would drive people to migrate to the new cpu something that
Alpha and PPC have not been able to do offering 3x or less performance
gains. I also think that Merced is the root cause of DEC's patent lawsuit.
If the Alpha is not the absolute fastest microprocessor on the planet it
has ZERO business case (not that that has helped Alpha compete against x86,
PPC, MIPS, PA-RISC or Sparc).

It will be interesting to see what comes of this.

WT

