> require three-phase power, and there is no workaround. 

However, the VAXstation 8000 is a small 8200 system in a little rollaround
box. Mine just uses a normal, average, everyday 120V power cord like any
other small machine would.

The VAXstation 8000 is a 6-slot VAXBI system in a little roll-around cabinet
about 2 1/2 feet high, 1 1/2 feet wide, and 3 feet deep. It has an 8200 
processor, some VAXBI memory, the DEBNT, ahd the MFM controller. They were
build for use by E&S in making a hot workstation; E&S added a backplane for the
graphics engine and a KA800 which, I believe, was used to drive graphics
engine. It's just a little tiny machine and shouldn't require more power than,
say, a MicroVAX II. 

I got one of my VAXstation 8000s direct from DEC to support the VAXBI work
the company I work for was doing at the time; we did a 4-port IEEE-488
controller and an interface to our MIL-STD-1553 stuff. About the time I
needed spares, the deal between E&S and DEC was falling apart and I was able
to pick up a spare machine cheap from E&S. The spare machine had the E&S
backplane and the KA800, but not the graphics hardware.

There was also a button box to go along with the machine; I saw one in 
E&S's warehouse when I snagged the machine. It gave you dials with an LED
display (presumably alphanumeric, like the ones used in other E&S machines
of the era) for each from a single serial port. Since I wasn't interested in
the graphics stuff, I didn't acquire a button box from E&S.

I had lots of fun with the VAXstation 8000; it made a nice little VAXBI
development system in conjunction with the VAXBI CP/M machine we made from
one of our IEEE-488 controllers...
