In <01bc694f$7dbb96f0$9e89d8cd@atlantis> "Scott Gilbert"
<scott@nospam.smoothmove.com> writes: 
>
>I think that Java looks like a really great language.  It seems to fix
many
>of the things I dislike about C++, and the syntax is familiar.  It's
not as
>easy as Visual Basic, and it's not as fast as C++.  It's major claim
to
>fame is that it has portable byte code, but since 90% of the world
(and ALL
>my customers) are in the WinTel market I don't care much if my
executables
>and libraries run on other platforms.


All >>your<< customers may be in the WinTel market, at least for the
moment, but your 90% figure is not for this world.  Mac & clones alone
take you to that, then add OS/2 and Linux.  IBM alone sells half again
as much in software as Microsoft's entire revenue - that's not WinTel
software.  Then there's Unix, VMS, and many others.  Some analysts
think there are still more terminals than there are WinTel PCs out
there (and a hell of a lot of WinTels running terminal emulation most
of the day).  

Inevitably, there will be native compilers for Java for several
platforms.  Best will be if 100% pure Java code can be distributed, and
any user who wishes can compile on the native compiler of his/her
choice and run that way where appropriate.  That would be a much more
disciplined market than we have today, but I don't see why it couldn't
be done.  

Native code of the fastest possible design will always be needed for
some applications, but surprisingly few, as Visual Basic has shown.  

Andrew Grygus  -  California Republic
