In article <01bc679c$f50a18d0$5b227392@glastonbury>, "dave porter"
<porter@$ultranet.com> wrote:

> Of course we'll care about speed. I've been programming since the
> days when a sub-1-MIPS CPU was a timesharing system for many users.
> A 200MHz Pentium Pro (allegedly 400 MIPS...) doesn't feel much
> faster to me, although I admit that my interface expectations
> have changed somewhat.
> 
> But my point, and I do have one, is that advances in CPU power
> seem to be inadequate to cover the (pick one according to
> prejudice) increasing incapability of programmers to program
> properly, or the the increased richness of the computing
> experience.
> 
> This is the Peter Principle applied to programming: code always
> expands to exceed the available memory. Run time increases
> beyond the time you're prepared to wait.

What you need is a discontinuity--a break in the pattern. This could take
the form of a quantum leap in hardware performance (like 5-10x faster over
a one year period) OR a quantum improvement in the code being generated.

There won't be a 5-10x improvement in hardware performance over a short
time period any time soon, so that leaves coding efficiency. 

OpenDoc offered the potential for this, but it doesn't look like it's going
to survive long enough to do the job. That leaves OO programming tools like
OpenStep. Conceivably, that could improve coding efficiency dramatically
over a relatively short time frame, resulting in an "apparent" gain in
software demands / hardware abilities.

-- 
Regards,

