Martin Knoblauch <knobi@munich.sgi.com> writes:

> > Hard drives are binary, they can only have two different
> > values: New and full. :-)

>  Actually, it is tri-state logic: new, full, crashed.

It is not always a quantum step between `full' and `crashed'.
There is an intermediate state of `crashing' where you hear the
whine of tortured bearings.  This state lasts an indeterminate
amount of time, but always ends before data can be copied.

This is Shroedingers data, sometimes called the Heisenberg certainty
principle -- you can be certain that your data will exist until the exact
instant needed.

// marc



