One thing which puzzles me.

With Intel's 8086 (aka Pentium) and "Intel Inside" campaign, it has
forced business (and the world) into buying 8086 architectures to run
the software. This is the same as the MVS shops locked in the IBM
environment because migration would be too costly.

This is one of the main reasons why DEC's ALPHA hasn't taken off.
Namely, it is not an 8086 no matter how fast it is.

Now, with Intel at the end of the 8086 and about to introduce a 64 bit
architecture, Digital decided to place the lawsuit which will probably
delay the replacement of the 8086 with the 64 bit chip.

However, the day that Intel announces the death of the 8086 and the fact
that folks will now have to switch architectures for their PCs (and apps
recompiled etc etc), will be the first day where DEC's ALPHA will be on
an equal footing with INTEL's 64 bit offering.

So, the quicker Intel introduces their new chip, the quicker people will
be forced to consider the cost/problems of switching to the architecture
and the quicker people will consider the more mature (and complete)
ALPHA chip as their replacement for the 8086.

The quicker Intel introduces its 64 bit chip, the bigger the edge
Digital will have on Intel in terms of available applications on their
chips. 

So, why force Intel to delay the death of the 8086 and its replacement
by a new chip ?



