OpenStep (referred to as "yellow box" above) is not vaporware.  It already
runs under: NeXT's portable Mach/BSD OS, Windows NT, and Windows 95. 
Apple/NeXT also plan to deliver OpenStep for MacOS.  The implication is
that you can write many kinds of applications with the OpenStep APIs, and
then easily deliver them for all of the operating systems mentioned
above.  This doesn't mean that it will necessarily be "one app for all
computers", but it does make things a lot easier on crossplatform
developers.

It's not a consortium, by the way.  IBM and Motorola are, to the best of
my knowledge, not directly involved (and I don't know why the original
poster even mentioned them).  Just Apple, which now includes NeXT after
the merger.

