And still work comfortably and efficiently? Yes, I am saying most
emphatically that I absolutely cannot.

The company I work for recently purchased an HP 9000. Its keyboard has
Ctrl in the home row. Commercial and non-commercial applications for
x86-based machines include utilities to swap Caps Lock and Ctrl. Freeware
utilities for swapping Caps Lock and Ctrl can be found on any large FTP
site. Provision for swapping these keys exists for every operating system
that runs on x86-based machines. Many replacement keyboards have switches
or other built-in provision for switching these keys. Search Usenet at
any time and you will find people asking how to swap Caps Lock and Ctrl.
Search the Web for documents referring to both Caps Lock and Ctrl and you
will find at least two guides to preventing carpal tunnel syndrome that
recommend using a keyboard with Ctrl in the home row.

Check out this page and some of the links on it for starters:

   http://bigbang.stanford.edu/~parkb/computers/mac/keyboards.html
          
or maybe try an AltaVista Advanced Search of Usenet for --

   (control or ctrl) near (caps-lock or capslock) near (switch or swap)

