dave porter wrote:
> 
> Of course we'll care about speed. I've been programming since the
> days when a sub-1-MIPS CPU was a timesharing system for many users.
> A 200MHz Pentium Pro (allegedly 400 MIPS...) doesn't feel much
> faster to me, although I admit that my interface expectations
> have changed somewhat.

You just hit the nail on the head. Your *Interface Expectations* have
changed. If you wrote the same program with the exact same interface as
10 or 20 years ago, it would run *alot* faster. But you (*we*),
actually, want easier.

Computers aren't measured in pure speed. Take a look at word processors.
That technology has been solid for some time. They just keep adding more
functionality. ("Whadaya mean we can't change the way our Word Program
works? Okay, then add something to it! Sell! Sell!" ) Even the interface
hasn't changed substantially in years. (They may move or add buttons and
menu's, but they're still buttons and menu's. ).

"Bigger is Better. I want one program that will do my word processing,
clean the house, and ..."

And that *attitude* is what causes bloat, apparent speed loss, etc.

 
> But my point, and I do have one, is that advances in CPU power
> seem to be inadequate to cover the (pick one according to
> prejudice) increasing incapability of programmers to program
> properly, or the the increased richness of the computing
> experience.
> 
> This is the Peter Principle applied to programming: code always
> expands to exceed the available memory. Run time increases
> beyond the time you're prepared to wait.

Or, in other words, The more we have the more we want. Quick quiz. Just
how much better *is* office 95 than 97. Guaranteed that the WP, Spread
sheet, and DB aren't much different. What else is there. Well, since MS
allready added the kitchen sink, they decide to integrate the water
heater. (And let's not get into the actual plumbing! ;)

But now you have more programs fighting for the CPU, and that's where
the speed comes in.

All IMHO, of course.

John Price
CHI Systems



