In article <1997May21.171439@news.all-net.net>, byer@mail.all-net.net (Robert Byer) writes:

:But, I don't have the "unlink" function which is kinda vital for this
:application.

   Under recent versions of DEC C under recent versions of OpenVMS, one
   has access to a wide variety of UNIX C library calls.  unlink() is
   not one, however.  unlink() is available via the POSIX API, though.
   Also available in both the C library and the POSIX API is delete().

:My question is, what is the equiv of the Unix "unlink" in GNU C v2.7.1
:for VMS or for that matter VAXC.

   unlink() can be used to remove links (aliases) to a file, and --
   when no more links are present -- to delete the file.

   OpenVMS has a "hard link" construct (SET FILE/ENTER, callable
   via the XQP IO$_ACPCONTROL interface), but the typical approach
   to "soft links" is the use of logical names -- a different, and
   arguably more powerful, approach.

   Whether you need to use a hard link, a soft link, or a simple
   delete, depends highly on the particular application...

