Paul David Fox <pfoxSPAMOFF@lehman.com> writes:

> Rob Rodgers wrote:
> 
> In 10 years time when CPUs are 10-100x faster than todays we wont
> care about speed. (I hope). And Java or whatever succeeds Java will
> be the norm and we wont care that the machine could be made
> to run 2x faster by careful optimisation.
> 
	That is the most laughable statement I've heard, and it's also
laughable how often we hear it. (I recognize you said "I hope"; and
I'm not picking on you.)

	I mean, 10 years ago, the statement was very common, and used
to justify the emerging X Windows, and the new 4GL's; at the time I
was sysadmin of a vax 11/780 which ran along at 1 MIP adequately
serving 40 users, all on ttys.

	Today with 300+ MIP machines available at 1/10th the cost of
the vax 11/780, these sleek machines keel over and die (I.E., the X
response drops to the floor) should someone log in over the network
and fire up simulation on your machine.

	It will never be too fast for anyone to care.  Programmers can
consume as much MIPS as can be provided. It's one of those self
defining constants. 

	A hardware simulation of a modern chip design always runs at
10 cycles per second. This is because if someone makes the simulator
faster, the the users will simply add more logic to the design or more
complex tests, until they hit the point of pain (which is roughly 10
cycles per second, by my experience)

	BTW, my experience is based on the fact that I was one of the
authors of VCS, a hardware simulator that ran designs 10 times the
speed of the competition, Verilog-XL. Users didn't tape out quicker;
instead they ran more tests, and built larger chips, using the same
amount of time they used to use before we introduced VCS in 1992.

	Outside the hardware simulation market, programmers will
simply add more realistic video to games, neater editing environments
(look at XEmacs!), more windowing options, et cetera.

