Hi!

Please can you help:

I just joined an underfunded organisation that has a Digital DEC pc 425
which has been out of action for the last few months, so I was trying to
resuscitate it.  Apparently people who were here before tried and failed.  

When it's powered up, the Resident Diagnosis chugs through its stuff ok,
until it gives the message:  

System Configuration Error - RUN SETUP.

It keeps going nevertheless, (VirusGuard doesn't detect any viruses)
reaching the C:> prompt.  Type Win RTN, and on the way it gives the
message:

"cannot find a device file that may be needed to run windows in 386
enhanced mode.  You need to run the Setup programme again.
vtcpip.386"

When you try to run applications (eg Word 6), there are problems,
especially with the A-drive.  

I tried using a recent version of Magic Bullet virus scanner:  if you put
in the first of the Magic Bullet discs in the A:drive and startup, it gives
a message something like:  (this bit isn't word for word)

"...CMOS reports that no A:drive, which may possibly indicate a virus such
as EXEBUG..."

It offers a choice of 5 different possible spec's for the A-drive, asking
you to pick the correct one, and then says it has fixed the problem, and to
restart with the MagicBullet disc in the A:drive again.  But the restart
just leads to the same "...CMOS reports..." message and so on.  

Running the fv86.exe (?) application on this first MagicBullet disc, I
scanned the c:drive, but no viruses were detected.  (Incidentally, running
mb_menu on this same disc, and telling it to scan the drives, it always
started by scanning the A:drive first, and invariably this lead to a hang
with the message:  "Bad Message.doc".  Message.doc is one of the files on
that MagicBullet disc.  I don't know what to make of this, since the disc
is completely write-protected?!)

I'm tempted to conclude that there might be a hardware problem, (or perhaps
in the cable to the a:drive, though that seems less likely, since the
machine used to work fine)?  The A:drive reads well enough that I was able
to run SETUP successfully, but afterwards, all the same problems as
detailed above were still there.

Does any of this sound familiar?  Any idea if it is indeed a hardware
problem, and if so, whereabouts?  Could it be repaired?  how much could it
cost to fix?

Please please let me know if you have any suggestions for the diagnosis, or
how to fix the problem.

Many thanks!

James



