From: dccoote@werple.mira.net.au (David Coote)
Subject: Realistic sustainable performance of single CPU Alpha systems.
Date: 1997/05/24
Message-ID: <5m68hn$rjg$2@eplet.mira.net.au>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 243547999
Organization: werple public-access Internet
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,comp.benchmarks


If you look at the clock speed and super-scalar capabilities of a 600MHz Alpha 
21164 you've theoretically got a chip capable of 2.4 GIPS. But unless you had 
an application that fitted - text and data - in L1 cache (thus having no 
requirements to access other memory in the hierarchy) and contained code that 
could run 4-way super-scalar each clock cycle, you won't see this performance. 
Real applications have branches, clobber the pipeline, don't have neat 
arrangements of 2 single cycle integer and 2 single cycle fp instructions 
ready for dispatching each clock cycle, won't fit in cache - whether L1, L2 or 
L3 - need to access main memory, saturate the available memory bandwidth, 
access (shudder) disk etc etc.

Most of the "reputable" benchmarks I've seen for the Alpha predict performance 
based on small applications that don't have a lot of relevance to large 
applications with a lot of IO written in languages such as C, Pascal or Ada. 

So what I'm wondering is if anyone has come across some benchmarks for the 
Alpha CPU comprised of real applications that use large datasets.

Thanks

David




