Stewart Stremler <stremler@rohan.sdsu.edu> wrote in article
<5m4b0i$qmi$1@gondor.sdsu.edu>...
> Rob Rodgers (rsrodger@wam.umd.edu) wrote:
snip
> "Efficiency" is an interesting concept -- efficiency measured how? These
> days I'm counting programmer-time, not run-time; it all depends on what
> sort of program you're trying to write. 

Efficiency in >>speed<< from the end user's viewpoint.  I have a cousin
who's an insurance agent.  Two different companies have the same "type" of
software app for calculating life insurance rates.  One take 3 to 4 seconds
to calculate the data, the other takes TEN minutes!  Same data inputted for
both.

Now, when he's on the phone with a client trying to adjust parameters for
the best policy, which package do you think he wants to use?  On one, he
can do several variations in a few minutes and the other is almost a
hopeless cause.

Neither he nor I care if the slow one took ten minutes to write, and the
fast one may have taken 6 months.

Don't ever forget that to the end user, time is money.

