Arun Sharma (arun@home.com.no.spam) wrote --

> As an end user, I'd rather have one operating system -- doesn't matter
> if it is from the evil empire -- dominate the desktop and the effort
> spent on making Java run faster redirected to writing better, faster,
> richer programs.
  ^^^^^^
Do I hear echoes of a CNN interview with A. Grub and B. Goat?

   A: "I would like to see the same idea turned into a more rich communica-
   tion tool...."  B: "....You bring in not only rich documents, but also
   audio and video...." "The Web is something that every year it gets richer
   and richer...." "But let's make sure that each of these protocols is being
   put into our products and that we participate in making those richer...."
 
As an end user, I am dead set against an operating system from any
commercial empire. These empires take advantage of people's ignorance to
put out inefficient, unergonomic, sugar-coated trash.

Anybody remember when the Ctrl key was in the home row, keyboard commands
were listed right on the screen, and a touch typist could work all day
without lifting a finger from the block bounded by the shift keys and the
top-row character keys? As an end user, I thought that was the greatest
advance in text processing since the invention of the typewriter. Then
the Mac came along: no Ctrl key. Then Windross: six bundled applications,
not one of which used Ctrl for any function at all. WordPerfect? A
handful of Ctrl-key commands, none of which was mnemonic in any way, and
one, count 'em, one of which was documented. Then IBM obligingly moved
Ctrl out of the main block, clone makers mindlessly followed suit, and
the task of neutralizing those with touch-typing skills was complete.

I'm all for standards -- REAL standards approved by 75% of the commercial
and non-commercial organizations participating in the standards process.
Standards, yes. Empires, no.

Dan Strychalski
dski at cameonet cameo com tw (not "twx" as in the header)



