Rob Rodgers wrote:
> 
> Jeff Fox <jfox@ricochet.net> wrote:
> [stuff on expectations growing faster than capability]
> >like.  100x todays machines is still way to slow for many things
> >we would like to do, so is 1000x so is 1Mx ...
> 
> Exactly.  Worse, there are _already_ plenty of things not related to
> cryptography and other non-directly-consumer-related tasks that could
> suck down 10, 100 or 1000x increases in computation.  We don't even
> need expectations to go up.
> 
> (126Mx would be sufficient for most things on my list, though,
> assuming the 433MHz 21164a as a baseline...)

I remember reading some of the speculations that Digital had with the
Alpha line, before making the first chip.
They wrote that going back 25 years, and compare speed of that times
processors with todays(1991) processors, would show a speed increase
of a factor 1000. They here after wrote that there was no sence
in beliving that this evolution would stop in the next 25 years,
and with that in mind they designed the Alpha arcitecture.
Bearing in mind that the tecnoligy evolution accellerates, it might
not be impossible that a speed increase of 100x in ten years
would succeed.

regards

	Anders Blicher Petersen

