> 
> It is obvious that Java will fail and not be adopted due to the lack of
> a native compiler.  Remember the utter failure and disappearance from
> the market of Microsoft's Visual Basic for just that reason?
> 

I disagree with your sentiment.  Programming languages form a very large
spectrum.  On one end you have hard to program, but down to the raw
hardware languages like C/C++.  On the opposite end, you have easy to
program languages where a great deal of what is really going on is
abstracted away from the programmer.

I think that Java looks like a really great language.  It seems to fix many
of the things I dislike about C++, and the syntax is familiar.  It's not as
easy as Visual Basic, and it's not as fast as C++.  It's major claim to
fame is that it has portable byte code, but since 90% of the world (and ALL
my customers) are in the WinTel market I don't care much if my executables
and libraries run on other platforms.

For me portability is not determined by the number of platforms I support,
but by the number of people using the platforms I support.  I don't like
Microsoft, and I have little reason to favor the Intel Architecture over
any other, but unfortunately I'm practical.

The reason I program in C++ (with some inline assembly here and there) is
because I want speed.  If I didn't care about getting the inner loop of my
rendering code to crank out 30 frames per second, I might very well use a
higher level language like Visual Basic.  (Unlikely, Visual Basic is one of
the ugliest languages I have ever seen, and I dislike it on principle. 
Maybe I'd use JavaScript or something, who knows...)

Java fills an area of the spectrum that is not currently valuable to me. 
If it compiled native and was fast as C++, I would switch immediately.  If
by some stretch of the imagination, it was only slightly faster, I think
many others would switch.





