Tad Rollow wrote:
> 
> My biggest problem is my complete unfamiliarity with the machine.  I am
> under the impression that this is the only VAX with this backplane, and
> know of no repository of net wisdom on the beast.  I've got a KA820-AA CPU,
> MS820-CA RAM, the DEBNT card and a TK-50, an AIO card (huh?) and a KA-800
> (dunno what that is either).  I've got the E&S hardware too but don't know
> what VMS is required to support it, nor what the required monitor should be.

I've never seen a VAXstation 8000, but some of the boards you describe
sound like the guts of a VAX 8200.

It's been a LONG time since I've looked inside one of these machines, so
take this information with a grain of salt.  But, as you said, it may
get you started.  If I am misleading you on anything, I'm sure that
someone will correct me.

It sounds like you have a BI backplane.  This was used as the system
backplane in the 8200, 8250, 8300, and 8350.  It is also available as an
expansion bus on the 6xxx series.

The KA820 CPU is about the same power as a VAX 780.  (I think it is
slightly slower, more like a MV II.)

The MS820-CA is 16MB.

The DEBNT is an Ethernet/TK50 controller.  There is a microcode upgrade
for it that will make it a DEBNA.  (Or is the DEBNT the upgrade of the
DEBNA?)

If I remember correctly, the KA-800 is a slave 8200 CPU.  VMS doesn't
run on it, but specialized software can use it.  Perhaps this runs the
graphics software.

If I remember correctly, the serial ports were disappointing in that
they could only run at 1200 baud or something like that.

You can probably replace the KA820 with a KA825 (VAX 8250) to get a bit
more speed.  The KA820 is about 1.3 VUPs.

The interesting thing about the BI bus is that you can stuff extra CPU
boards into the system to make a multiprocessor.  There is some
configuration (cabling?) needed for the extra CPUs, but I don't know
exactly what is involved.  2 KA820s gives you an 8300 and 2 KA825s gives
you an 8350.

It is quite possible, however, that the graphics head will not work with
multiple CPUs.

If you want to get really wild, 3 or more CPUs should give you an 8370,
which DEC never officially released.  DEC used a 7 CPU version of this
as one of their test machines for the SMP code in VMS 5.0.

Good luck!

