>Rich Adams wrote:
>> 
>>   Just because it's Wintel today doesn't mean it's going to stay that way.
>> Java/NC is looking very promising.
>
>Java? NC's? I don't know. IMO, they have a chance at the corporate
>level, but can succeed at the consumer level only if marketed at a
>communications tool sort of like a super telephone. I do see NC's
>rapidly rising in cost as users push the devices upwards in performance
>and capabilities to match what they have lost with with full fledged
>systems. Used as a modem "dumb terminal", I don't see them going
>anywhere other than replacing scores of 3270 and 5250's in businesses. 

	Actually, a "super telephone"...   Hmmm...

	If these suckers can be a speakerphone/picturephone (due to
	improved backbone/local loop bandwidth) they've a chance at
	penetrating the home market.

	Especially if, within the house, there is a LAN, so more than
	one is present (imagine using one cable and having multiple
	conversations through it).

	The NC needs to get it's foot in the door;  Consider it a
	pretty face to a "family" computer (a "family" level of
	file/application server).  For instance, I'd like to have
	a system providing files, etc, for my family and allow my
	wife and child(ren) simultaneous access to their home
	directories.

	Yes, I'm atypical, but consider that the NC is merely a
	brown paper bag over TCP/IP (sure, it's a *pretty* paper
	bag, but consider the ugliness of the TCP/IP protocol stack).
	If we only use it as an access point (like a telephone) and
	it ain't sold as a "computer", well, maybe there *is* a future.

	Otherwise, make it a component of the stereo.  Make it a
	driver for HDTV monitors (when *they* happen).  Make NCs
	a front-end for programming your VCR.  Make NCs talk to
	the garage door opener.

	There is a future for NCs...  ...but I don't think it's
	_as_ a computer...

