I agree with Ken, VMS backup is bullet proof. If your read 
the docs and don't do anything real dumb. (As we all have 
once upon a time... 8)  Just two hours ago or so I had to 
restore an old job we did with our first VAX.... and 11/730 
with vms 3.??... must have been 15 years ago... 9tk tape 
that left so much goo on the heads...anyway vms backup did
a couple of group recoveries and all is well.

More to the point... I wish a "VMS backup" was available on
win-NT.  I can live without GUI... I think I have now
looked at everything available for NT and still have not 
found a real good backup system.

3rd party solutions for something as important as preserving 
the system scare me.  Once upon a time I had a PC backup 
program called PCTOOLS... this piece of junk would do a
read verify pass on a saveset and declare it good... but
it used an in memory cache of the tape contents... turned 
out that if you had more than 16k files (or some such 
number) the content directory written to the tape was 
corrupt and you could not access the tape. The company said
it was a know bug and they would be happy to send us the new
version at DISCOUNT!.... this became the backup program for
ms-dos somewhere along the line... and PCTOOLS was a number 
1 seller for more than a year.

--Doug




