People seem to also think that because of JIT performance isnt
an issue.

In 10 years time when CPUs are 10-100x faster than todays we wont
care about speed. (I hope). And Java or whatever succeeds Java will
be the norm and we wont care that the machine could be made
to run 2x faster by careful optimisation.

As it is today, one tends to find ones programming habits change:
Put 128MB in a machine and you dont worry about the odd
4k overhead for this and that. Put 1GB in a personal games machine
and you wont care about losing 20MB to this and that....

And 1GB only covers us for about 2 years. By 2001 we will probably
have 4GB RAM, and we will start to work differently. (Still Office '3
will require more RAM than we have because then we'll have 3-d textured
editing using a 747 flight-sim to move the mouse and we'll be
zooming in at the atomic level on the fonts to shove the electrons
around to get a better anti-aliased display at normal
resolution....) 


