Hi Folks:

     We're planning on buying a dual AlphaServer 4000, running Windows NT
4.0, with Digital Clustering for NT 1.1.  It will be used in a software
and hardware development group as the primary file, print and SQL 
database server.  The environment is Parametric PRO Engineer and AutoCad
for mechanical design, ViewLogic and PCAD for circuit board design and
MicroSoft Developers Studio, InterSolv PVCS and Tracker on the Software
side.  Our requirements and expectations are not great.  We are just looking
for a reliable setup to provide near continuous access to software source
code, mechanical and circuit design drawings and access to the bug tracking
database.  Nearly all our applications run on the client.  Some need to
connect to a license database that resides on the server and the only
application that actually runs on the server is SQL Server.  We are also
using office applications like Word and Excel.  90% of the environment will
work with an AlphaServer, because we are currently running on it.  The last
10% is ViewLogic and we are testing it under FX!32 right now, but it's too
early to tell.  About 20% of the clients are Alpha NT and about 80% are
Intel NT and WIN95.  If the ViewLogic evaluation doesn't work out on Alpha
clients, we will switch to Intel clients.  No matter what the client is, I
would prefer to use Alpha NT at the server level.  

     My question is this?  Is anyone out there running a similar environment
already?  For my purchase order to be approved, I have to include contacts
at sites that are using Windows NT Clustering on Alpha.  Our CFO will be
asking alot of questions, before signing and I have to do the homework before
bringing it up.  Our DEC Sales rep is working hard on this for us but isn't
getting very far, finding us reference sites to call.  I checked the WEB
and there did not appear to be any success stories at www.digital.com that
specifically mentioned NT clusters.  I did see that one of the "monkey" ad 
finally mentioned Digital Clusters for NT.  I even saw it mentioned that
MicroSoft Exchange will gain clustering support sometime that year.  That's
the final piece of Software we are using in our environment.  However it
is supported by the Corporate side of the business, not engineering, so it's
less of an issue with me.

     I don't follow this conference religiously, so if this issue has
already been covered before, I'm sorry.  Thanks for any comments.

