In article <5ls2mo$p05$1@news.th-darmstadt.de>,
    Uwe Leinberger GSI Darmstadt <leinberger@axp621.gsi.de> wrote:
[...]
>Just think: one hour of fiddling around "to get it working", or data lost, 
>or downtime, costs typically at least $70/person/hour. [...]
>
>It quite seems that this is possible ONLY in case of computers. I can't imagine
>that anyone would accept something like this in case of a car, dish washer or
>vacuum cleaner. Or for a milling machine, lathe or some such. 
>
>BUT WHY THE HECK DO PEOPLE ACCEPT ALL THIS IN CASE OF COMPUTERS?
>AND FORGET ABOUT ALL REAL RUNNING COSTS IN CASE OF COMPUTERS?

    I'm reminded once again of the 'Flash Gordon' tale - one of those
B&W movie serials, there was one scene where the crew had to abandon
their rocket ship. First they had to get their anti-gravity belts,
which were belts with little boxes that might be anti gravity belts in
the 25th century or something. Then they had to get their ray guns,
which were little handheld technical wonders which could possibly be
futuristic weapons.
    They they had to get the portable radio - a huge wood console that
took two people to lift it. Because everyone knew that this is what
radios looked like - they were big, heavy, with lots of complicated
dials.
    Today it's computers. Everyone knows that computers are stupidly
designed, crash every 20 minutes, lose data, and will collapes
entirely if you try to upgrade them. Anything that actually works and
is easy to use obviously isn't a *real* computer.
    Mindset is a *very* powerful force - someone's first experience
with computers will pretty much be the *only* thing they think of as a
computer without a lot more experience.

--
