On 10 May 1997 19:28:50 GMT, brettiam@aol.com (Brett i am) wrote:

>>I have recently come across an old tape back up drive (TK50) by Digital
>>and was wondering if anyone had a program that I could use in order to
>>make it comapatible to my home computer that runs Win95.

	Unless that's a SCSI version of the TK50 (in an enclosure, not just
the bare drive), what you ask is not possible without costing you more
than a new tape drive would.

	The TK50's have a native capacity of 100 MB. Their interface is,
outside of a few external-type SCSI units that DEC made for some of the
VAXStations, completely proprietary, and no controllers for them were ever
made for the PC market that I know of.

	Further, Windows 95 lacks native support for SCSI tape drives. You
would need to use software that is specifically designed to run SCSI
drives under W95 (such as Novaback, Seagate Backup Exec, or Cheyenne
Backup).

	My suggestion is to look for a different drive and trade or sell that
one off to whoever has a DEC system that needs one.


