Java? NC's? I don't know. IMO, they have a chance at the corporate
level, but can succeed at the consumer level only if marketed at a
communications tool sort of like a super telephone. I do see NC's
rapidly rising in cost as users push the devices upwards in performance
and capabilities to match what they have lost with with full fledged
systems. Used as a modem "dumb terminal", I don't see them going
anywhere other than replacing scores of 3270 and 5250's in businesses. 

From my point of view the main selling points of x86 PC's are price and
flexibility. There is no other platform that offers the performance for
the low price of PC's. Adequately decked out PII/266's are under $3000,
and I expect P55C/200's will hit the $1500 point before to long. At the
same time, these boxes can run just about anything from custom/vertical
apps, office productivity, 3D graphics to games (not always the best
choice, but just fast enough) and scores of operating systems. 

It will take a VERY compelling platform to challenge x86. I thought that
4-16 way SMP boxes using cheap PPC 603e chips might make a decent
alternative, but PPC self destructed with Apple. Alpha is another
high-performance alternative, but the majority of users don't want to
give up the compatibility of x86 for a 2-3x speed boost. 

All the best,

