I was thinking of the 32 bit setup VMS had for handling errors.  I
can't remember the setup exactly, but the low end bits signified the
error, the middle bits signified the ident and an offset to the error
text, and the high end signified the facility.  Of course, you could
create your own errors using a high level definition file and their
error compiler.  Then you could use VMS's error handling which would
then handle your own defined errors as it would normal systemic
errors.

>>
>>VAX Clustering - What Bill's trying to give us now with NT servers,
>>except 10 years later.
>>
>>VAX Scan - Brillant parsing language.  When I asked the salesman for
>>it - before they gave it away to DECUS - he hadn't even heard of it
>>(and, sadly, nor has most of the DEC population.  I don't even think
>>there is a OpenVMS port).
>>
>>etc. etc.  Heck, even MicroSlough borrowed DEC's COM architecture.
>
>Don't I wish!  *.COM used DCL, a very high level batch language.  Nothing
>like MS/s .BAT.  The *.COM that MS has are nothing like the *.EXE in VMS.
>The *.EXEs in VMS are obviously NOTHING like those in MSDOS(which carries to
>win NT).  The needed data is quite different, and the endians are different.
>

Umm - I meant Common Object Model (COM), as opposed to the "dot COM"
(.COM) DCL files.  Although, you did remind me of some of the ideas I
thought were good by DEC but just never caught on.  For example,
parameters passed BY DESCRIPTOR (as opposed to passed by value or
reference).

There were other things.  Source code control.   I've used RCS, SCCS,
DevMan and PCS - but I still think CMS and MMS are better.  The ol'
VMS debugger was awesome for its time, considering it was for
character based terminals.  The language sensitive editor (LSE), vax
object library and linker.  FMS?  All those wonderful SYS$ calls you
could have made except that the example for the function you wanted to
use was in BLISS.  Why does HTML always remind me of Digital RunOff
with extensions? (FX: gasps for air and moves off soap box)

I'd better stop here and try to calm down! :)

>I never heard of vaxscan, but DCL has a parser builtin.  Use that, as EVERYONE
>has it!  Open VMS HAS to have it.  If it didn't, most(possibly all) VMS programs
>would need to be ported!
>

I think you're thinking of the DCL lexical functions eg. F$PARSE.  Vax
Scan is an actual language (like COBOL or C) except designed for
parsing.  It can be likened more to LEX and YACC.  Less powerful in
some ways, better in others (has language support for trees) but
*much* easier to use  - for example, to describe a grammar it itself
uses a grammar that is easier than regular expressions, but just as
powerful (reminiscent of BNF) .

I won't even try to finish on topic.  My head says Intel to win, my
heart says DEC.

