> columns of the following table from that site.  Perhaps
> Tom has added more info, or revised his tables, but I
> did not infer a clear K6 win from this prior evidence.
> 
> WINDOWS 95
>                        K6    Pentium Pro  Speed-up  Normal.
>                        233       200       for K6  for clock
> =========================================--------------------
> Business Winstone97   54.8      52.4      +4.6%     -10.2%
> Highend Winstone97    23.6      24.8      -4.8%     -18.3%
> Business Winmark97    98.1      84.2      +16.5%     0.00%
> WinQuake 1.09         15.5      23.4      -33.8%    -43.1%

The figures are practically meaningless. All RISC processors
underperform on business applications if all you do is wind 
up the on chip clock. The Pentium II samples show similar 
effects, a 266MHz PII is only 4% faster on "real" benchmarks
than a 233MHz part and the 300MHz part is only 3% faster than
the 266MHz one.

Increasing the chip clock speed does not improve overall 
performance unless the bus speed goes up as well. Most PCs
run their off chip bus at 60MHz or thereabouts. So a cache
miss becomes very expensive. Given a choice between a 266MHz
part with a 256K cache and a 233MHz chip with a 512K cache
I'd always chose the latter. Part of the reason for the
speed of the AXP machines is that they can use a wide data
bus.


Before writing off DEC's chances of getting Pentium IIs 
consider that DEC have their own fab capacity that is 
probably sufficient to meet their PC needs. They were
making 486s a few years back and may well have an existing
agreement to manufacture newer designs. Intel may not have 
that much choice in the matter. Alternatively they can 
probably obtain any number of chips on the open market
without revealing who they are.

I don't think this really hurts DEC. Intel can try to
exclude them but that only forces them deeper into the
AMD camp. Their engineers might well be able to tweak
the K6 design to keep it competative with Intel if the
dispute is not resolved early.


So far DEC are being relatively mild in their approach.
I would have filed for injunctions in multiple 
jurdisdictions and filed against the importers as well
as Intel. The US courts are unlikely to act in a way
that could damage the US economy, don't believe that
high placed officials won't make phone calls to judges
when there is a $100+ billion a year industry at stake.


If Intel is forced to stop its fab line for a week they
lose staggering amounts of money. The knock on effects
could possibly cause the US budget to be damaged as
seriously as if GM, Chrysler and ford all had a strike
at the same time.

On the other hand DEC could be bought outright for only
$5 billion, patents and all. Intel probably could not
get away with it themselves but they could give someone
else the money, allow them to take over the company and
asset strip the patents. That would take much less time
than the lawsuit would to resolve. If Intel would like 
to cut me a check for the sum in question I can probably
raise whatever premium the market imagines DEC is worth
over its current price in other ways. They get the patent 
rights and an uninterrupted market. I keep the company,
sounds fair to me (at least as an opening bid, I might 
be open to arguments that the sum be treated as a loan
in certain circumstances).


I'm disappointed at Tim May, if this is his idea of "rough
stuff"... 


	Phill Hallam-Baker



