>>
>>  If DEC is so full of great ideas and can implement them, why aren't they
>>more profitable than Intel?
>>
>
>My experience of DEC - oops, Digital - is that they have great
>engineers and bring out products way ahead of their time - and have
>one of the lousiest sales forces I know.

They USED to have products that had OLD technology, and were EXPENSIVE!
NOW, though everything is better, they got rid of most of their independant
salesforce!  They told them they needed to sell more, have more red tape,
a lower profit margin, DEC competition, and less help!  NOW, it might be
better, but it did get pretty bad.

>Points in case:
>
>VMS  - pretty good OS for those of us who worked on it.  Well thought
>out (compare its help with Unix's man pages - and its error handling
>beats just about anything else), consistant, *well* documented (BTW,
>any MicroStuffed fans please note: your WinNT (one letter ahead of
>VMS) had some input from the VMS crew).  But, sadly, proprietry

Its help could be better.  UNIXs MAN pages are more easily changed, and date
to a VERY old standard.  It isn't THAT consistant.  Some times you have /log=
sometimes /out=, and sometimes NO method!

Error handling?  What about constant underruns, and various other problems?

The error handling was one of the things I DIDN'T like!

>
>VAX Clustering - What Bill's trying to give us now with NT servers,
>except 10 years later.
>
>VAX Scan - Brillant parsing language.  When I asked the salesman for
>it - before they gave it away to DECUS - he hadn't even heard of it
>(and, sadly, nor has most of the DEC population.  I don't even think
>there is a OpenVMS port).
>
>etc. etc.  Heck, even MicroSlough borrowed DEC's COM architecture.

Don't I wish!  *.COM used DCL, a very high level batch language.  Nothing
like MS/s .BAT.  The *.COM that MS has are nothing like the *.EXE in VMS.
The *.EXEs in VMS are obviously NOTHING like those in MSDOS(which carries to
win NT).  The needed data is quite different, and the endians are different.

I never heard of vaxscan, but DCL has a parser builtin.  Use that, as EVERYONE
has it!  Open VMS HAS to have it.  If it didn't, most(possibly all) VMS programs
would need to be ported!

Steve





