In article <337E2523.23706EA8@informatik.tu-muenchen.de> Bernd Paysan <paysan@informatik.tu-muenchen.de> writes:

> Patents are a way to monopolize ideas (copyright is another, trade
> secrets a third).

Don't confuse the concept of intellectual property with "monopolizing ideas".
Trade secrets do not protect you from independent reinvention, and unless I'm
mistaken, if you intend to sue somebody over them you must show that the
defendant has actually "stolen" them from you.

Only patents come reasonably close to monopolizing ideas.  Copyright doesn't
cover ideas at all, only their expression.  And even then it allows for public
quoting, limited reproduction, and implied permission.

The missing link in your list however are trademarks.  Obviously you can't
trademark a name like "Windows" in a context where the word already has a widely
accepted meaning (such as computer software).  But you can trademark an existing
name like "Java" in a different context, and stop others from using it for their
computer-related products without permission.

Trademarks don't cover actual ideas either, but in practice there are claims to
complete expressions such as "Java compatible(tm)" -- which will probably hold
as long as nobody bothers to challenge it.  It definitely won't stand in court
though; and if it ever should, I suggest that Bill Gates trademark "Windows
sucks" and save himself a lot of bother.  :)


Jeroen


