Alberto C Moreira wrote:
> 
> In article <5lgd4v$m5j$1@excalibur.flash.net>, RokNroB@Flash.Net says...
> 
> > The Patent system is supposed to protect your idea from being copied
> > for free and sold.
> 
> I thought ideas couldn't be patented, while inventions could. For example,
> my belief is that you cannot patent the idea of having a cache, although you
> can patent a device that caches. But if I invent a different device that
> also caches, but does it in a different way, I don't think I'm infringing
> your patent. Am I wrong ?

Yes and no. You can patent the caching concept with the title "Apparatus
to accelerate memory accesses using a special memory device that saves a
plurality of previous fetched memory items" (or however it would be
expressed in this ******* patent-english); certainly you should take a
time machine. A cache obvious is an apparatus, and it is an advance over
some previous state of art. If your claims are vague enough, all
currently used caches fall under this patent (but because you had to go
so far back in time, your patent would have expired). Vector queues,
however, don't, since they accellerate memory accesses by remembering
values that will be needed in future.

If your claims are not vague enough (e.g. you defined a direct mapped
cache), another cache (fully associative) could not infringe your
patent, and a set-associative cache might be a borderline.

On the other hand, you are right: You can't patent the idea of caching,
as using previously obtained data to predict current needs. This is a
much broader concept, and the cache is only one application. Branch
prediction is another, you use the statistics of each branch to predict
whether it will branch in future. You can only patent things that are
specific enough to be useful, and you must say for which purpose (e.g.
retrieving memory data fast or avoiding pipeline flushes after branches,
not both). If you find another purpose for the same idea, you can patent
it, too (e.g. if someone patented LZW for saving disk storage, you can
patent LZW for reducing network bandwith requirements, or vice versa).

It's another question how to handle patents, and this is a political
one. Patents are a way to monopolize ideas (copyright is another, trade
secrets a third). Intentionally, patents should expire fast enough so
that the original inventor has a chance to make his profit, while the
community can use the invention after a reasonable delay. The invention
cycle in computer industry is much shorter, therefore the delay isn't
"reasonable" here. Therefore, lots of patent interchange agreements
exists in computer industry, however, only between equal partners.

Thus most of the time, patents save the stronger from the weaker, which
is not as unintentional as the naive may expect. Intel is bashed because
of not playing fair (or fair enough) in this league.

-- 
Bernd Paysan
