Ultrix off of a TK50 tape on a remote Vax, ftp it to a Linux box, and from
there dump it onto an old QIC tape drive. Then unplug the QIC drive, carry
it over the the VS, and (hopefully) install Ultrix.

The QIC drive is an old Archive + an Emulex MT-02 SCSI1 interface, 
scrounged from a Sun4/260. Linux on my 486 has no problem with it. It's
QIC-24, so the whole contents of a TK50 won't quite fit (only 60MB)... 

Coming from the TK50 on the remote VAX:
	dd if=/dev/nrmt0h of=ultrix.boot bs=512
	dd if=/dev/nrmt0h of=ultrix.boot2 bs=512
	dd if=/dev/nrmt0h of=ultrix.rootfsys bs=10240
	(etc, etc)

Going to the QIC on the Linux box:
	dd of=/dev/nst0 if=ultrix.boot bs=512
	(you get the idea)

All of this seemed to work just fine, aside from running out of space
at the end. With the QIC drive moved to the VS,

>>> show dev
gives us something like

  VMS/VMB  ULTRIX  ADDR		DEVTYP	NUMBYTES  RM/FX	 WP

  DKA0	   RZ0	   A/0/0/00	DISK	104 MB	  FX	 
  MKA400   TZ4	   A/4/0/00	TAPE	.......	  RM	 WP

Yeah, the disk is puny, but it's finding the tape just fine @ scsi id 4.
When I try to boot,

>>> boot mka400:

  MKBTDRIVER halting at relative address 0CFB
	... (huge register dump, etc)
  84 FAIL


The tape's in, but it never moves. Hmmm... I toggled the Emulex
board's switch for using SCSI parity, but that made no difference. 

Any ideas?

Do I need other boot parameters? (what are they?)
Does the VS firmware simply choke on this drive? (why? what works?)
Will I encounter something bizarre like an endian-ness screwup, since I'm
writing the tape on an i486?

