If DEC's case is based on the press release then they have already lost.
Intel could be refering to anything in those quotes, and my first
reaction when I read them was that not enough new research was coming
out of academia not from other cpu companies.

Intel has been adding risc technologies to the x86 line for years now,
and I think they have hit the wall with how far they can take the x86
ISA. So they move to VLIW which is fairly new and which they hope to
leapfrog risc to a certain extent. 

I am not a big fan of Intel, but I think DEC is on shaky ground with
this. They have been unable to compete against Intel in the mass market,
have no consumer presense (the largest growing segment), and they have
even been uable to compete in the workstation market against the likes
of Sun or HP who have slower systems.

The best thing that could happen to DEC is that Intel buys the company
and pays shareholders a tidy profit over the market value of DEC shares.
Alpha would then be in a position to become Intel's 64-bit solution and
would become the de facto standard for the post-x86 world. Intel would
benefit buy losing a competitor, gaining a PC distribution channel that
could be sold off, and gaining the world's fastest cpu. It would come
out of the gate with solid MS support unlike Merced. With Intel support
DEC's binary translation software could probably get enough speed, and
OS-level support from MS to make the transition from x86 painless. 

From DEC's point of view, if any CPU eclipses Alpha performance then
their whole business case is shot. DEC does not have the share of any
market to weather it. 

All the best,

WT



