William Taylor wrote:
> From DEC's point of view, if any CPU eclipses Alpha performance then
> their whole business case is shot. DEC does not have the share of any
> market to weather it.

You fail to understand today's market (as does Digital). It is not
"world's fastest chip" driven. It is price driven.

Only a few customers actually, really, really need the fastest chip.
Most others can do with multiprocessor systems that rival DEC's alpha
offerings in performance but cost a lot less because they run
off-the-shelf software.

When Alpha came out, it was WAY AHEAD OF ALL OTHERS. Yet, it didn't make
any big waves. It was too expensive because it was sold by Digital.

If DEC came out with a 1 million MHz Alpha tomorrow, it would not give
Digital any added market share. It may make weather bureaus happy (cause
they would be able to afford such a beast). But Intel would still be
able to advertise that they have the fastest chip in the world and go
unpunished.

It is about time that Digital's legal department woke up. They should
have been concentrating on DEC's competition a long time ago instead of
concentrating on DEC's customers with fancy rules and business practices
that have cost DEC so many customers.



