I'm not familiar with the earlier RISC architectures, but most of
the stuff claimed in this suit seems to me not basic RISC design
stuff, but performance improvement that are (for obvious reasons)
just as compatable with CISC as RISC, although perhaps offering
slightly more improvements in the latter.

Did those early RISC CPUs do things like keeping a likely branch
address stack, or do branch prediction? Did they keep old values
of changed registers so one could `roll back' several instructions
should a branch be predicted incorrectly? These are not basic
architecture issues, but performance enhancements, and there's
certainly not a lot of pressure to use these sorts of techniques
until processors grow significantly faster than memory and even
their own second-level cache.

