: >microprocessor now.  "Nothing left to copy" refers to paradigms, of which
: >there have been no new ones.  What Grove/Barret was saying was that with
: >current paradimg pushed to the limit, new paradigms are needed, and old
: >ones no longer suffice.  They were actually hyping a bit the new Intel 
: >architecture lab, and talking about Intel's effort to now step in, and
: >lead and define new architectures.  Ofcourse now, Palmer twists it a bit,
: >and use it as the "smoking gun".
: >

: I gotta disagree with your assertion that there are no new paradigms left
: in computer architecture.  All sorts of stuff are being invented in the
: computer field that do not fall into typical von neuman architectures.
: The only difference is that people don't call them CPUs, they call them
: ASICs.

Okay, what I meant to say was that the current paradigm is pushed to 
the limit, and lots of "new paradigms" come and go, There hasn't been 
a new paradigm that I've seen that made me go.. "Hey, that's 
neat, why didn't I think of that, ofcourse, that's the way to go!" 

: Yes my fellow internauts, there's a revolution going on in computer
: engineering as we speak.

I'm slightly more pessimistic, I've seen quite a few "new" ideas that
were either 

1. not very new.

or

2. disconnected from reality.

: Processors have traditionally been designed as a basic fundamental
: component to be used as a building block for a larger, unspecified
: system.  But with these days of advanced CAD tools and ASIC/Gate
: Array/FPGA technologies with rapid turnaround times, the concept of the
: fundamental building block of a system has been erased!  System designers
: can freely work down to the transistor levels from the top of the system
: down with the ease of writing a few lines of VHDL code or putting together
: a few circuits in a schematic, and then haveing the system automatically
: create a new chip!  No need for a small army of Layout engineers.
: Simulate, and then if it works, make it!

I just finished writing VHDL code for a single precision FPU.  Pain in 
the butt.  That being said, it is a lot easier to have a HDL to code things
up than to have to draw everything up by hand.  However, when you want
the highest speed and/or density in the circuit, nothing compares to the
human mind.  Computer programs follow an algorithm, they cannot deviate.
Human beings can follow an algorithm, as well as make cross boundary 
optimizations where it makes sense.

I'm also doing some FPGA programming, it is my opinion that if you want 
to do reconfigurable computing by using FPGA's to compete against top
of the line CPUs, you may be sorely dissapointed. The FPGA's have a lot 
less number of gates, plus the dynamic routing costs you circuit delays,
resulting in a lower clock speed than if you fabbed it.  Don't get me 
wrong, I love FPGA's, but I don't believe that they'll be ushering in a
new era of computing paradigm any time soon.

: The new top down model allows for systems to be optimized for their
: functionality, using ASICs to speed up the system.  Such ASICs can
: be interfaced to a traditionl CPU/memory system, such as highly
: specialized 3-D chips or network interface chips, or they can form the
: foundation of an entire system, such as specialized DSP's.  Chips are
: being created today with more and more specialized functions on them that
: have traditionally been left for software on a general purpose CPU to
: handle.  Eventually, even the general purpose CPU itself will become
: specialized for a role.  You're going to see CPU's that start to become
: more like operating systems.  Earlier efforts like multi-threading CPU's
: underscore my point.  There so much room for advancement if you look at
: the CPU this way!  You might even have a microkernel on a CPU, with
: multiprocessing, specialized I/O along with memory, special processing
: hardware, and so on...  These are all a result of looking at a system from
: the top down, and then creating a chip, just for that purpose!  The
: classic definition of an ASIC.  If you look at just a traditional von
: neuman architecture CPU, then sure, you're stuck at advancing the design!
: But when people look at the big picture, creative advances can be
: made everywhere.

We think alike in some ways. I believe that multiscaler, multithreaded
CPUs may have a good future. Interthread communications to be handled on
a chip, programmable flash memory holding the microkernel in the CPU
itself, there are a lot of room for improvement.  However, all of this
is not part of the current paradigm, and needs lots of research.  It is
my belief that this is what Grove/Barrett meant when they said that
"there is nothing left to copy".  All the good ideas in the big Iron from
the 1960's have been used. Now, we enter a new world with multi-hundred
Million transistors per chip, segmented clock distribution network, etc.
Hence the need for Intel to create their own architecture lab to do this
sort of research, and lead the industry forward.  Palmer turned that
statement into proof that Intel has done no research in the past, but 
merely copied Digital's ideas.

: I've had a job where I designed a system that would be much faster than
: anything else, even an Intel Teraflops computer.  Except that it would
: do one thing, and one thing only!  And there was no need for a main CPU 
: for something like that.  I just took a C program, and implemented it in
: hardware!  And it looked NOTHING like anything else.  It didn't have a
: Program counter, it didn't have a "main memory" (and therefore it didn't
: need a cache), and it didn't have an instruction set.  But it did its job,
: and quite quickly, too. :)  And it sure as hell didn't cost the millions
: it took Intel to make its slow and overweight monster!  

Well, can you convince others to use and/or purchase this computer to
do any sort of general purpose work?

: Who needs a CPU? THE CPU IS DEAD!  LONG LIVE THE SYSTEM!

You were doing so well :)

: -Bobby





