>>> On 16 May 1997 01:26:55 GMT, RokNroB@Flash.Net (RokNroB) said:

[ ... ]

RokNroB> The Patent system is supposed to protect your idea from being
RokNroB> copied for free and sold.

No, because the patent system confiscates the right of people who own
their inventions to use them commercially in their own products; the
patent system forbids everybody but the person that publishes an
invention (not an idea -- ideas are unpatentable in the USA, as in most
countries, even if many USA courts seem to ignore that) to use it, even
if it is their own idea.

[ ... ]

RokNroB> If it was your sweat in that patent, would you feel still feel
RokNroB> the same way?

Essentially all ``inventions'' contain a vast majority of somebody
else's sweat, and very little of the inventor's sweat. The process of
invention is almost always one of incremental, tiny, improvement, on
somebody else's work and copying somebody else's ideas. This is
particularly clear in computer/CPU architecture, where almost all design
consists of applying textbook principles or fairly obvious combinations
