Not realy. Most minies in the late 60. early 70. ( until the PDP11) was
much inspired by PDP8 (or PDP5), but the 4004 having code and data in
different memory using 4 bit for data with 16 + 4 digits and i think
it used a subroutine return stack ( of 4 levels ??, or was it the 4040)
 made it VERY different from a "real" mini like PDP8, HP2100, Varian or
the first DG /Nova.  
I wrote a simulator for the 4004/4040 on a PDP8 back in 73/74, and i
remember the 4004 to be different, very different. The 4040 was much 
more of a "real" computer. 

But to the Intel research level. The 4004 was as i remember it an "ASIC"
order for a set of calculator IC's, made into a more general processor
by the development team. Hardly basic research.

