> If you look at the i960, you might see that Intel is pretty good
> at designing instruction set architectures, having learned many
> lessons from the iAPX432.

  The i960 looks like an ISA designed by a committee.  And although
  Intel markets it as a RISC chip (like the RISC integer unit in the
  486 ;-) it is more like a twisted hybrid like the Fairchild/Intergraph
  Clipper.  Given the multiplicity of addressing modes and instruction
  formats, along with some of the more baroque instructions, it is seems
  that its designers hadn't gotten over their fetish for microcode.

  It was rather successful when introduced simply because most other
  RISC vendors were hot for workstation class CPUs rather than <$100
  price points for embedded.  The existing i960 family members are now
  suprisingly stale in performance and price/performance.  Its hard to
  be competive against MIPS's semi partners and DEC fab 6 when all you
  get are the hand-me-down 0.8 um (0.6 um soon?) fabs from the x86 crew.
  Given Intel's justifiable preoccupation with the lucrative x86 market
  I would vote for i960 as the embedded architecture most likely to next
  follow the 29K in walking the plank.  What was the last major i960
  design win?

Paul

all opinions strictly my own.

