Although I do not beleive Digital to be smart or powerful enough for
this, perhaps this is part of a bigger picture:

Intel is not the only manufacturer of 8086 derivatives. AMD and CYRIX
also make them.  Most large companies now depend a lot on PC sales
because their minis/workstations are just too expensive compared to
Wintel.

There are eyes wide open on the Wintel monopoly. As soon as their
monopoly becomes too evident, the USA government will pounce on them and
somehow force the dismantling of this monopoly.

Perhaps the Digital and Cyrix moves are part of a greater plan to
de-stabilise Intel and break the monopoly it has on personal computers
and servers. Perhaps we will see other computer industry players make
moves against Intel in the coming months.

Think about it, Intel is about to switch from its glorified 8086
architecture to a 64 bit architecture. So switching from the 8086 to
whatever Intel provides next may be just as hard as switching to a MIPS
64 bit chip or a DEC Alpha chip.

If Intel starts to get bad press and an image of a copycat, and the
others finally get some recognition for their R&D, it will give others a
chance to break onto the market of the next generation personal
computers since Intel may not have an assured monopoly position.

Again, I doubt that Digital is smart enough to lead such a bold a subtle
move, but it is nevertheless a possibility of a certain portion of the
industry quietly ganging up against Intel.



