> I remember the i860 coming out, and Intel hyping it, and some people
> believing it -- until they tried to run real code on it, and found that the
> exposed FP pipeline was a real pain, and you had to use FORTRAN (and even
> special FORTRAN libraries) to use it.  I also remember OS people looking at
> it and thinking, "Gah!"

Untrue.  Having been an OS person who did two i860 OS ports,
I don't know why people would say that the pipeline was
a problem, the procedure for the OS context switch (which
is the only thing I could say was tricky) was documented.

As for applications, you didn't have to use fortran.  Intially
you pretty much had to use assembler (until the better C compilers
came along).

Intel's main problem with the i860 was underhype and the
fact that the chips didn't work.  The errata sheet (which
was like pulling teeth out of Intel to get and still wasn't
complete) was many pages long and most of the pain and suffering
on the i860 was dealing with where it differed from it's
own specification.



