Ron Natalie <ron@sensor.com> writes:
> Intel's main problem with the i860 was underhype and the
> fact that the chips didn't work.  The errata sheet (which

I believe there was also the problem (already mentionned) of compilers:
yes, the chip was impressive when used smartly, but Intel didn't seem to
realize that RISC is about considering CPU+compiler together, so they applied
RISC principles without having the compiler to "back'em up". The compiler
technology only came later (too late).

Also the visible pipeline was an error similar to the branch-delay: convenient
for the first implementation, but a huge pain in the ass for the following. It
was not designed as an ISA but as a single-implementation chip. I'm all for
getting rid of compatibility constraints between CPUs (by imposing
compatibility at some other level), but it hasn't happened yet (apart from
AS/400, at least) and for sure wasn't about to happen when the i860 came out.
It basically had no future from the start.


        Stefan



