I bought one of these AXPpci33 motherboards, and I thought those
here might be interested in my experiences with it.

First, you'll want to get the latest AXPpci33 guide from ftp.digital.com;
the one that comes with the board is quite out of date and has
incorrect pinouts for the serial port. The SRM console is also
available from there if you need to flash that in.

These models do appear to support the 233 MHz CPUs; I took the
21066A out of my Multia and dropped it in, and it's now been working
on a full system rebuild for nearly two hours without problems.
(The manual says that you need an upgrade kit from the factory to
support the 21066A.)

The memory bus is, like all 21066 machines I've seen, pretty poor,
and this hampers the performance of the machine. Don't expect this
to provide the performance you get from an Intel P100 system.
However, memory is still significantly faster than a Multia, if
you've been considering one of those.

This also means, of course, that you probably want to put as much
cache in the machine as possible. The 128Kx8 chips for a 1 MB cache
are hard to get. Alliance still makes them, however. I bought the
15 ns parts, but I had severe problems at the 15 ns setting; I
switched back to 20 ns. and things started working ok again.

You need a power supply that also supplies 3.3V only if you have
a 3.3V PCI card. I've never seen one, and certainly anything designed
to go into a PC will not need 3.3V.

Note that the spacing of the keyboard and mouse connectors on the
PS/2 version is not standard. I had to go at my case with a pair
of tin snips.

Overall, I'm pretty happy with the thing. It's a long way from a
300 MHz 21164, or even my P133 system, but it makes a fine file
server for home use. (I run NetBSD on it.)

