Paul Ayers <pay23@netcom.com> writes:

>> What professional programmer does not have VC++, or couldn't afford it?
>> There are over 1800 native Alpha/NT apps available right now, and another
>> 1000-1500 being ported currently. You could also run Linux, but I'm not
>> sure about Alpha/Linux apps.

> What is the availability of Alpha/Linux apps like?  Is an Alpha/Linux
> combo possible (with a good number of apps)?  

Depends on what you're looking for.  Of course you'll find ports of most
freeware or source availability stuff, and EM32 can run binary-only
Intel/Linux apps -- rumour would have it that they run well, but I
haven't had a chance to try things out.  Last I looked you could also
run Digital Unix applications, but for dynamic libraries you need the DU
ld, and the legal implications are unclear.  I don't think you're in
trouble if you have a DU licence, and I don't think Digital is going to
come after you anyway (their legal team probably buzy, eh?), but hey,
I'm not a lawyer. 

Since ''professional programmer'' is mentioned, the weakest part of
Alpha/Linux might be compiler technology (gcc produces slower code than
Digital CC), and perhaps the beginner-unfriendlyness of XEmacs as an IDE
(Though why a ''professional programmer'' can't be bothered to learn
effective tools with a learning curve is beyond me)

Microway and others supply their own compilers, that might be better
than GCC (anyone have numbers?)

Anyway, you can always dual-boot to NT and/or Digital Unix.

~kzm
