In comp.sys.intel Maynard Handley <handleym@apple.com> wrote:
: In article <5ldtsc$itr$2@excalibur.flash.net>, RokNroB@Flash.Net (RokNroB)
: wrote:

: > Wasn't the iAPX 432 modeled after IBM mainframe's and minicomputer's.  If I
: > remember
: > correctly, even the data book stated this!

: Certainly it's not modeled after System/360.

It was the endpoint, the culmination of the CISC paradigm. Instructions
varied in length substantially (I believe there were variants, including
prefixes and suffixes, which were over a 100 bytes long) and could accomplish
anything under the sun. It was more VAX than VAX, and more 360 than 360.

However, it probably collapsed because ADA compilers generated faster code
(by more than an order of magnitude) for the 16-bit 80286.

Undoubtedly, Intel did a lot of basic computer science R&D on this project.
It must have convinced them about what *not* to do in future projects, and
the i860 (started in 1985 and introduced in 1989) in some ways represents
the antithesis of the iAPX432 paradigm.

Anyway, the i860 predates the Alpha and has some of the disputed ideas at
issue in this suit. Also the VLIW/LIW paradigms, the Berkeley, Stanford and
IBM work would cover most of the rest as prior art. I doubt that anything
would survive after this.

siva@emi.net



