DEC has a long history of micro processor chips.  It starts with the
CMOS version of the PDP-8 (intersill 6100) as a compaction and cost
reduction of the PDP8, later there was a performance improved version
the 6120. this was the mid 70s.

DEC also did this for the PDP-11 with the LSI-11 back in the mid '70.
The LSI-11 was followed with the F-11, J-11 and t-11.  The J-11 was
noteable for I&D space and cache and an external FPU in 1983.

Not long after was microvax-1 chip set, and shortly after that the
Microvax-II vax on a chip. Cvax aka microvax-III up the performace
nearly 3x by the 1990 time frame. and alpha protos were nearly
working.

What is missed is the 9000 series machines (and others like 8650) that
did it all on lots of chips (ECL).  THere is a long progression of
research and products to support the idea that DEC was reducing their
R&D to practice.

Allison



