On the evils of patent laws...

1. patents are vague on purpose
	for example, if you mention silicon, someone can do
	the same thing in gallium-arsenide without violating
	the patent.

2. you can patent nearly anything
	The wording of patent laws are vague and anything beyond
	the common knowledge of a professional in the area can
	be patented.  So if you define the area as electrical
	engineering, almost everything in a specialty area, which
	would not be common knowledge to all EEs, is patentable.

3. trial by jury
	Yes, patent law trials are by jury.  So you have 12 
	non-technically knowledgable persons deciding what is
	an extremely technical matter.  And don't expect the
	judge to know too much more than the jury.  (Although,
	there was an interesting precentent recently where a
	judge hired outside experts to tell him what's what.)
	The trials usually become trial by analogy - each lawyer
	picks an analogy in common place things that makes his
	side look good, and tries to convince the jury his is
	the right analogy.  Essentially, if it goes to jury,
	no one can predict what happens, so most companies take
	the sure bet and settle out of court.

4. receiving a patent can take a while
	While I was at Motorola, they received a patent on low
	power devices that they had submitted nearly a decade 
	ago, and then went about suing anyone who had used that
	technology.  So if some of the things DEC is suing on
	sounds simple, they may have been recently given on
	old submissions.

As always, insults, corrections, and praise are welcome.

Cheers,
