Seems to me that a disclosure of an Intel patent app on the net a few
days ago showed that their planned IA64 will NOT have, for its fast
instruction set, another x86 variant. Looks like the chip will run x86,
but run another presumably "native mode" instruction set to gain its
speed.

If that holds up...and of course one has few guarantees...it would mean
that current Intel apps may be irrelevant.

There was a problem we used to notice back at RCA when we ran VAX
11/780s (which had native and compat mode hardware) that if anyone
ran compat mode jobs they tended to dominate cpu usage. The machine tended
to run at the speed of the slower instruction set until the fraction
of the slower instruction set used became VERY small.

The app covered instructions to enter and leave x86 mode and a few
register copy between architectures instructions. (The former
capability seems prefigured by one of the mil-std architectures
to me...ayk-15? Could be another...that had an implementation defined
instruction with variable number of args that could have been used
to add another instruction set. We discussed that at RCA way back...
though that was it.)




