Paul DeMone wrote:

>   No one says Intel doesn't have some of the best semi/process know-how in the
>   business but when it comes to processor and system architecture well the
>   x86 ISA speaks for itself.  At least they had professional help on IA-64.

Let's not forget that many aspects of the x86 ISA became
locked in over 20 years ago, when the vogue of microprocessor
design, and the intended applications, were very different.
Old versions of Foley and Van Dam advocate segmentation,
and I have an article by a FORTH programmer (ca. 1982) that
sings the praises of the 8087 stack architechture.  The
emphasis on raw micro speed wasn't that important, back when folks
believed speed was the job of mainframes and minis.

Remember how ludicrously undrpowered the first commercial
RISC systems were, e.g. IBM RT ca. 1985 -- ten years after
Intel started designing the 8086?  Even in 1985, the real
estate of RISC chips was so limited that no one dared include
such niceties as on-chip FP hardware (the RT used a Nat Semi
32081) and hardware integer multipilcation, or even good hooks
to an FP coprocessor.

When the i860 came out in 1989, it blew away the competition,
and the i960 gaves us ludicrously inexpensive semi-RISC
CPUs for embedded designs.  (Oh yes, I forgot the persistent
belief that the i860 was not really an Intel design.)  Not bad
for a company that can't design microprocessors.



