Also, we all see now that the 432 was a flop. (This has been discussed many
times in comp.arch, that I have seen. The 432 was harshly criticized by
Dave Patterson, of RISC fame, as early as 1980-1.)

My point was of course not to defend the 432 as an example of prowess, but
to use it as a counterexample to the "Intel has never done any research,
has never done anything original" nonsense we're seeing spewed in the wake
of the DEC lawsuit. Whether the 432 was a flop, a travesty, an interesting
experiment, or whatever, it was surely a huge project for Intel. (It was
also a very secretive project.)

And in fact the hugeness of the 8800/432 project had some important
implications for the x86  architecture. Intel expected the 8080A and 8085
processors to be eventually replaced, for higher end uses, with the 8800
(later renamed the 432, or the "iAPX 432"). It became apparent in 1976 or
so that the 8800 would be further off in the future than many had expected,
and a crash program was started to fill the gap. 

(This was nicely described in a recent "Byte" article.)

A few architects were put on this gap filler and were given only a few
weeks to come up with an instruction set and architecture. The rest is
history.

Possibly, even probably, this was unfortunate. The billion dollars Intel
spent on the 432 and Gemini and related systems (the joke was the joint
Intel-Siemens "Biin" company stood for "Billions invested in nothing")
could better have been spent on devising a super clean architecture. But
hindsight is always 20-20. The same could be said for nearly any company,
that mistakes siphoned money and time away from better (in retrospect)
projects. IBM spent vast amounts of money on "FS," and so on.

> Old versions of Foley and Van Dam advocate segmentation,
> and I have an article by a FORTH programmer (ca. 1982) that
> sings the praises of the 8087 stack architechture.  The
> emphasis on raw micro speed wasn't that important, back when folks
> believed speed was the job of mainframes and minis.

And there are lots and lots of other factors to consider besides just
architectural elegance and adherence to currently popular features. 

(I hesitate to start listing them. But here are just a few issues: in the
mid-70s, the cost of  640 K of RAM was staggering. And the success of Unix
and C was not apparent to many. (Yes, some of you will volunteer that you
saw these successes...good for you.) And I doubt anyone at Intel expected
the x86 architecture to be dominant for more than 20 years. And so on.
Manufacturability is of spectacular importance: IBM almost certainly picked
the 8088 for the IBM PC because the architecturally more elegant 68000 was
much larger, had 2-3x the number of transistors, and had not yet been
converted to a CAD system...the 68000 design existed only as very large
sheets of rubylith, and IBM was understandably concerned about future
interations.)


> When the i860 came out in 1989, it blew away the competition,
> and the i960 gaves us ludicrously inexpensive semi-RISC
> CPUs for embedded designs.  (Oh yes, I forgot the persistent
> belief that the i860 was not really an Intel design.)  Not bad
> for a company that can't design microprocessors.

I hadn't heard the belief that the 860 wasn't an Intel design....it was
certainly off the beaten path of x86 designs, but I knew the design team
and I never heard charges they copied the design....

The 960, of course, owes aspects of its design to the 8800/432/Gemini/Biin
effort, though there are big differences. 

--Tim May

