In article <337B5DAF.37E1@swcp.com>, H.W. Stockman <hwstock@swcp.com> wrote:
>When the i860 came out in 1989, it blew away the competition,
>and the i960 gaves us ludicrously inexpensive semi-RISC
>CPUs for embedded designs.  (Oh yes, I forgot the persistent
>belief that the i860 was not really an Intel design.)  Not bad
>for a company that can't design microprocessors.

Wow, I don't remember any of that.

I remember the i860 coming out, and Intel hyping it, and some people
believing it -- until they tried to run real code on it, and found that the
exposed FP pipeline was a real pain, and you had to use FORTRAN (and even
special FORTRAN libraries) to use it.  I also remember OS people looking at
it and thinking, "Gah!"

And the i960 was predated by the AMD 29k, which was the market leader until
relatively recently (about five or six years ago, I believe).

The 29k was a nice chip, I remember that.  Tons of registers.  Far too many,
really ;).  With "register windows" through software convention, extremely
fast traps.  No byte access instructions at first, though -- they added
them.  (And then DEC did the same thing with the Alpha... I'd've thought
people would have learned :).)

I think it's very obvious that Intel *can* design microprocessors.  And
they've done a rather impressive job in keeping the x86 up to speed.  Of
course, when they sell 45 million units, at a cost of several hundred
dollars apiece, they can afford to throw manpower at solving problems -- and
generally will be able to.

I find myself rooting for DEC in this suit.  Mainly because I'm very worried
about Intel, and think something desperately needs to be done about them.




