| 
| Wow!  And here I thought Intel's success could be traced to IBM's
| decision to use Intel hardware in the first IBM PC. Amazing...a
| history of Intel's success that makes no mention of Microsoft.

One of the major factors for IBM choosing Intel CPUs in 16 bit
flavors was that virtually all of the "personal computer" software
at that time was written in assembler for the 8080. The S100 systems
came out in 1976 (or so) and were the most widely used systems.

The first uP O/S widely used was ISIS, written for and by Intel. It
was on their development systems. Gary Kildall wrote CP/M, which was
more user friendly, and I believe Intel turned it down (not sure on
that one). So CP/M became the first O/S of the masses, and there was
a Cp/M-86 for 16 bit machines. But Kildall supposedly didn't
negotiate well with IBM, and they turned to Microsoft. MS was able
to buy QDOS from Seattle Computer who wrote it to sell their 8086
S100 hardware. The rest is history.

Point being that the decision to run Intel hardware was based on
being able to (mostly) translate existing 8080 assembler to 8088
assembler to jump start the available software. So Intel didn't just
get handed the contract, they had taken action earlier to make Intel
16 bit CPUs very attractive for the application.

Motorola and National Semi both had vastly better CPUs from a point
of being able to generate code with a compiler, but Intel went with
the design which leveraged the existing software and won.

If the change to 16 bit happened earlier, CP/M might not have become
a big thing. Had it come a few years later, more software would have
been written in C, and porting would have been less of a problem.
Had AT&T been clever they would have licensed UNIX to IBM for next
to nothing and the whole world would have been on one operating
system. Intel did the right things and was in the right place at the
right time. Had the demand for security been greater, a 2nd
generation 432 could have been done in a year or two, and probably
would have been 5-8x faster. Intel didn't quite have the fabs to use
enough gates and speed things up, but they seem to have gotten
better processes shortly after.

It wasn't all luck, or even a major part luck.
