Tony A. <tony-a@ultranet.com> wrote:
| >As a poster in comp.arch stated, it's difficult to imagine that
| >Intel would knowlingly violate Digital's patents. 
| 
| And why is that?  Integrity?  Fear?  

Sounds right to me. I can't imagine anyone in management allowing an
infringement, because it would be a very costly mistake, not only to
the company but to the career path of the manager. Making a mistake
like that is going to result in doubt about your judgement, while
doing it deliberately could lead to a job flipping burgers. It could
*possibly* result in a suit by the company against the employee,
particularly since "deliberate" infringement is claimed and a jury
might believe that an employee did something against company policy.

| Computer architects are very well read and well informed.

Get real. They are about as well informed as any other worker in a
highly technical field, and I can think of some I know who prove
that. There are informed people in any field, but I don't think chip
designers are exceptions to the rule, one way or the other.

There are two facts which have not been disputed yet: Intel and DEC
talked about Intel licensing technology, and Intel didn't do it.

One explanation for this is that Intel decided that the patents
were either (a) so broad that they wouldn't stand, or (b) so narrow
that Intel could work around them. Take your pick. I've read a few
articles which seem to confirm that the patents are very broad and
fuzzy, which is good only to a point, after which a good lawyer will
be able to argue that the patent a "vague" rather than "broad,"
which I gather means it may be invalidated. There was certainly
prior art in branch prediction and caching, so the DEC patents have
to cover some unique way of doing those things. If they are so broad
they cover unpatentable concepts they might be either narrowed or
invalidated, in which case the whole world could use the technology.

A patent lawyer told me that with some "broad" patents it was
better to take a small royalty without a fight than go to court and
risk clarification. Also, most companies will pay a small fee to
avoid a large legal fee.

If Intel claims the patents are invalid, then DEC will be playing
for very high stakes indeed!

Of course, if I ran Intel I would look hard at just buying DEC and
avoiding the issue. Given the choice of a decade of legal spending
to an uncertain result, or a quick sale for top dollar, I know what
I'd do. Investing is not a social tool, at least for people who want
to be able to afford to retire ;-)
