| I'm not too familiar with the US legal system, but did anyone else 
| notice that the text of the lawsuit ends with
| "Plaintiff demands trial by jury on all issues."
| Is that normal, and what are the implications? Anyone?

Sure, it means they hope to convince a jury that they are a little
company against a big company, and get some sympathy. May well help,
too. Of course if they bore the jury to death with technical issues,
the jury will get to the point where they are willing to give money
just to not listen to any more.

Look at the OJ case for an example. The prosecution totally
overkilled the evidence, explained the meaning of statistical
information so poorly it was confusing to people who understand it,
and the defense portrayed a multi-millionare mass murder as a
victim.

I suspect Intel has better lawyers, and hopefully a judge who will
cut the garbage.

The issue will probably be resolved in a week or so without a trial,
however. If a judge gives DEC an injunction, DEC can drag the trial
out for a decade, while Intel has no income. If the judge doesn't
give an injunction, Intel can delay the case while DEC spends money
it doesn't have.

The other thing is that Intel may take a deep breath and buy DEC. If
the case ran on for years Intel would lose lots of money, but
there's no given that DEC would make more. And by the time the suit
was settled Intel might not have enough money to pay anything. DEC
hurts Intel with no sure gain. If Intel made a good offer for DEC
the stockholders would probably take sure profit now (or an Intel
stock swap) over "maybe money" later. And Intel with the DEC patents
could build even better CPUs than they do now.
