In article <5lg9ru$4o9$1@news.fm.intel.com>, rtowle@pcocd2.intel.com (Rich
Towle - MD6-FDC) wrote:

> Well, if you are looking for a specific event that led to Intel's
> success, I suppose you would have to say that THE event was
> the decision of Bob Noyce and Gordon Moore (perhaps the two most
> influential figures in the IC inductry) to found the company.

Of course, actions and decisions over the next 20 years were just as
important.  I was there from 1974-86, so I missed the first few exciting
years. But by 1974 there were still massive challenges facing Intel (TI,
Fairchild, Motorola, American Microsystems, Advanced Memory Systems,
Mostek, etc....many of these no longer are recognizable to most readers of
these newsgroups).

Moore and Grove were the biggest influences I saw.


> And, of course, I heard that IBM didn't make their decision by
> tossing a coin with Moto on one side and Intel on the other.
> 

My recollection is that IBM felt the Motorola 68000 was the more elegant,
regular design, but that the size and complexity, and Motorola's lack at
that time of design tools, made the 8086/88 choice a safer one for a
high-volume product.

(The 68000 was, in 1979-80, extant only on rubylith (think: X-acto knives
cutting Mylar films), whereas Intel's designs were in CAD form. And the
68Ks transistor count was 2-3x that of the Intel part's count, meaning
manufacturing yields would likely be quite a bit lower.)

--Tim May

