In article <337da19d.3384747@news.demon.co.uk>, brian@shapes.demon.co.uk
(Brian Drummond) wrote:

> It seems to me that, given high memory costs, and the way programming costs
> were perceived at the time, the ISA of the 432 was a thoroughly brave stab
> at a solution. But technology at the time delivered only a tiny fraction of
> the horsepower (or even the pin count) to implement the ideas properly

I agree. Now we see some flaws, but at the time it seemed reasonable.

And, interestingly, the reasons are _greater_ today than they were then! (A
surprising statement, I agree.)

Remember that no so long ago a corporate or university computer cost
perhaps 20-40 times the average annual salary of the programmers who worked
on it. (E.g., in 1970 a 360/75 might have cost $300K and programmers were
being paid $10-15K a year. Even in 1980, a VAX sold for $100-150K or so,
and programmers were earning about $20-30K.)

Today, a programmer earning $60-100K (give or take) typically works on a
machine costing $3K for a Pentium type of machine, and maybe  a bit more
for a Sun or DEC type of workstation.

(As a sometime consultant, I've earned enough in a single day of work to
buy a quite capable computer. Times have sure changed.)

One would think, then, that the programmer, who is now the expensive part
of the equation, should be supported by the highest-level tools, the best
GUI/environment possible, and the highest level languages devisable. (My
last job at Intel, from 1984-86, involved just such a bet...I bet that
ultra-high-level tools, like LISP machines, would come to dominate. Well, I
predicted wrongly.)

Instead, we have $100K a year programmers writing C code on $3000 boxes.

Make of this what you will.

--Tim May


> >The 960, of course, owes aspects of its design to the 8800/432/Gemini/Biin
> >effort, though there are big differences. 
> 
> Which aspects?

I'm not an expert, but the 960 was done by many of the folks who did the
432/Gemini, and at least some of the 960s offer the "tagged architecture"
(for protection, etc.) features the 432 was touting.

