                 Primer on DEC's lawsuit against intel

I don't know about the rest of you, but I've spent a significant
fraction of this day cruising news sites, gathering as much info as I
can on this whole issue.  Since some of the posts in this newsgroup
seemed tp reflect a small amount of confusion over the facts, here's my
understanding of the sequence of events, and my own interpretation of
them.

1990/91 
Digital enters into negotiations with Intel to license its Alpha
technology to Intel.  Talks break down and both parties walk away. 
As is standard, these negotiations were condusted under the
protection of non-disclosure agreements. From what I gather,
Intel engineers may have had a peek at Alpha technology very 
early on.

1991/95 
Digital is consistently and conscientiously maintaining its
cultivated image of a brilliant R&D house, with little capacity to 
execute, while building up a services business.  Intel meanwhile is
making _gobs_ of money, releases Pentium et. al.

1995/96 
Faint rumblings in Bob Palings in Bob Palmer's head that something may 
be wrong with the speed of Intel's development and the advances made as 
the Pentium Pro is released.  Intel fucks up big time with a floating
point calc. flaw in the Pentium, hides it from customers, then denies
the extent of the problem, finally has to back off.  Just over a year
later, everyone has forgotten the incident, and everything is fine and
dandy (short term memory?).

Aug. 96 
Andy Grove interviewed by WSJ where he lets slip some incidental
comments, basically suggesting Intel had cappitalized on 2nd mover
advantage, but now was concentrating on original R&D because it
had jumped to the head of the pack (whom did he think he was kidding,
Intel still makes a mass makret chip, nothing in the Alpha class). This
sets off bells in Palmer's head.  Now he sets a bunch of people on 
this issue, to see if DEC has a case at all, and where the great
advancements Intel made came from.

1997
Intel continues to make gobs of money announces MMX (generally
considered to be a marketing gimick) and later Pentium II.

May 12, 1997
DEC files lawsuit against Intel, alleging 'wilfull infringement' of DEC
patents (10 are supposedly listed in the suit, which I haven't seen,
they were issued between 1998 and 1996) which cover a wide range of
technology.

May 13, 1997
DEC stock rises a bit, Intel stock falls.  Cyrix announces it's also
suing Intel for patent violations involving the Pentium Pro and
PentiumII line of processors.  Cyrix was very quick off the bat, the two
patents it's suing Intel over were only granted earlier today.

	ANALYSIS

After reading a bunch of news stories, some views, and some more
opinions on this newsgroup, here's what I have to say about the whole
thing.

I'm not a chip designer, don't know the first thing about them, I
wouldn't be in a position to evaluate the technical merits of this case
(in fact, if anyone is intimately familiar with both Pentium and Alpha
architectures, I would love to hear from them). I don't know whether DEC
will win this case, that depends on too many factors for me to evaluate
with too little information.  What I do know is that Digital makes
amazing chips (just about every technical eval. of the Alpha I've read
agrees it's an amazing processor) and that Intel makes mass-market
chips.  It seems entirely possible that Intel may have incorporated some
DEC technology into its chips.

I don't know whether this is a good or bad thing.  One part of me wants
to hurrah Intel, since it's obviously made faster machines available to
us at a lower cost.  Then there's another voice in my head that says
there's nothing to show that DEC or some other chip manufacturer/
mass-producer might not have been able to do the same (I doubt DEC would
have succeeded at this, the company lacks the sort of marketing pizzaz
Intel and Sun have).  Regardless, I think the fact that DEC made
substantial investments in the development of Alpha processors entitles
them to remuneration for this.  Considering the fact that DEC was ready
to license Alpha technology to Intel (which now, with hindsight seems
like a good idea, since DEC is an excellent engineering shop.  But one
never knows what Intel culture might have done to DEC), if there is
cause to believe that Intel then turned around and used DEC technology
without informing or compensating DEC, it seems like they were playing a
really fucked up game.  If it is true that Intel used patented DEC
technology without bothering to license it, then I think DEC should be
compensated.

Palmer says DEC has taken so long to come out with this because of two
reasons.  First, this whole thing sounds so outlandish that it never
would have occured to them.  Second, Palmer wanted to be absolutely sure
about this thing before coming out and slamming Intel.  Supposedly DEC's
internal evaluation has convinced Palmer that there is a very strong
case here.  I don't think DEC is making a last wild grasp at Fortune
200dom (DEC is not currently a Fortune 200 company, the last list I saw
had it down near 460).  DEC could have sued Microsoft's ass over NT, but
chose not to (on the other hand, it may be that DEC learned from that
lesson, since NT hasn't really paid of yet and people like me are
annoyed at what happened to Mica anyway.  But it's apt that MS picks up
DEC's dregs).  Nevertheless, I'm inclined to trust DEC's internal
evaluation to a great extent.

There are many aspects to this whole thing.  Right now, the entire focus
is on the PR stuff. So Palmer is talking about the WSJ article, sudden
realizations, etc. which sounds like a compelling story to the public.
This whole thing, however, will be decided in court, and when it comes
down to that, the judge is hardly going to accept that Grove's vague
comments in a WSJ article are acceptable testimony.  So the issues 
during the trial are going to be very different.  I presume there will 
be a lot of tech talk, and a number of patent lawyers are going to
become quite rich (the engineers who come in to provide expert testimony
probably won't get much, too bad).

DEC is looking to win this suit, and I must say I'm cheering DEC along
all the way.  I don't think this is a trumped up suit, and I like DEC
products and have believed in DEC ("Whatever it takes" still sounds great
to me, but notice it's "engineerish").  If DEC wins, then as Palmer
says, the payoff could be quite good.  DEC wants an injunction against
Intel's continued use of its technology.  Which means Intel may have to
redesign the Pentium line if it continues to sell those chips.  DEC also
wants to be compensated by three times the amount of estimated economic
damage to the company (or perhaps it's Intel's profits from the
use of DEC technology, sort of like a retro-licensing fee, but I doubt
this, though can't say for sure without reading the actual text of the
suit.  Which incidentally, won't be available except through the
Massachusetts court reporter's office).  This sum could run into
billions of dollars.  Also, Intel could be seriously hurt by this case,
especially if it means they have to redesign some of the PentiumII and
PPro lines.

Palmer is also making this into a "We're battling a monopoly" thing.
Obviously he wants the sympathy most of us have for the underdog.  I've
forgotten how many times he said something like 'Intel used DEC
technology to strengthen its x86 monopoly'.  That's again for our
benefit, I don't think it's going to be relevant in court.  Well mine he
has, I don't think I like Intel at all.  There's also an element of
Intel invading DEC's turf.  From Palmer's comments I gathered that they
were concerned with the inroads Intel was making in the hingh-end
micro-processor market.  With PPro's especiallyy.  It almost sounds as
if "well it would have been okay if they'd continued to make
sand-castles with Pentiums, but now they're talking workstations, and
we're going to whup their ass".

As for the market analysts, most of them, in my opinion, are pissing in
the wind.  I don't expect any reasoned or fair evaluation from them.
For one thing, few of them are aware about how micro-processors work
(most are probably as ignorant as myself), and none of them are
dis-interested.  Intel is a major tech stock, and has a huge market
capitalization.  I don't think there's an investment house out there
that would like to see the ground fall from under Intel.  In any case, I
think statements like 'DEC couldn't handle Intel in the market-place,
and this is a last attempt of a dying company's to salvage itself' are a
bunch of crock.  So I'd take whatever you heard from the newest pretty
boy at the hot investment houses with a considerable grain of salt.  The
market ouperforms over 90% of them anyway.

If Digital does win this case, win this case, not only will they have a
large pool of cash available, but Alpha will have received the media
attention a product like it deserves.  Digital might just be poised to
take on a surge of consumer demand for Alphas as the superior performance
(and price) becomes clear.  Still, I don't think Digital's fortune's
rest with this case.  Digital makes the finest Unix around, developed
what's probably God's own OS (VMS), has the finest micro-processor on
this planet and has a services business that is phenomenal.  DEC isn't
going down anytime soon, they're too good.  On the other hand, Intel is
in a commodity business, it's great strength has been in building a
mass-market brand-name, reaping the rewards of scale, and mass-production
(another case of the Fords making oodles of green, while the Bentleys
and Royces have to make do with browns and beiges.  But at this point,
in microprocessor terms, anyone can own a Bentley-Alpha).  So I don't
think Intel as such is a revolutionary company, it was just able
to arbitrage it's position into a very lucrative business.  DEC on the
other hand has been phenomenally influential in the development of
computing, right from when Digital's minis began to cut into the
mainframe business and time-sharing slowly destroyed batch processing. 

I'm ignoring a lot of larger concerns here.  At some point, this could
be an issue o an Intel engineer who saw something really good in the
Alpha design, and thought of ways to use those concepts.
Microprocessors arecomplex logical structures, as such almost as much
works of nature as they are of humanity.  One wonders whether nature
itself is patented.  But this is something that comes up in almost every
discussion of intellectual property rights, and we've arrived at some
agreement that broad, general ideas are not patentable, but particular
processes are. 

In sum, I'm with DEC on this one, and probably all the way.  It's just
criminal that the lawyers will make so much money out of this.  I still
think DEC stock is a great buy (even at 35, obviously it was a better
buy when I last considered it, at 26) and I wouldn't be surprised if it
tripled by this time next year.  Too bad I don't invest.

