I don't think that I ever read that WSJ article, however, I would agree
with what was quoted by those Intel executives on the big picture.  There
really hasn't been any grand new computer architecture ideas in the last 
25 years.  All Intel has done is looks that the ideas they used on big
iron 25 years ago, and see what was applicable to microprocessors, and
then implement them.  Everyone does this.  What Digital's suit alleges
is something a little different.  Digital alleges that Intel willfully
violated some specific parts of the architecture advancements that they
have made in the last 8 or 9 years, including stuff that no one could have
known about, except Some Intel Engineers were supposed to have been 
exposed to it under NDA back in 1990 or 1991.  Digital is alleging that
those Intel Engineers may have gone back and implemented those ideas on
the Pentium and Pentium Pro, etc., thus giving Intel the huge performance
boost.  

If the suit is based solely on the WSJ article, it'll go no where really
fast.  I am waiting to read the actual patents, and the architectural features
that was supposedly violated.


