Yeah, marketing has a little to do with Intel's success just as it has
a little to do with DEC's lack of success.  Far more relevant were the
brilliant and gutsy business decisions of Intel's management back in
the early to mid 1980's.

In the midst of a painful slump in the American semiconductor industry
Intel's leaders made two risky decisions that essentially insured the
company's position at the top of the computing industry.  First, they
decided to focus their efforts on building the highest performance x86
compatable microprocessors without relying on a second source
manufacturer.  Second, they decided to invest heavily in world class
manufacturing capacity.  Both of these decisions were essentially
betting the farm on what they thought was the future of computing.

That future, as they saw it, was a migration from the then current
mainframe and mini dominant world of business computing to a new
desktop paradigm.  They saw it correctly.  DEC and IBM didn't.

Intel was wildly successful well before the Intel Inside(R) campain
came to light in 1991.  In the late 1980's most people were using
Intel products on a daily basis without even knowing it.  Intel
microcontrollers were dominant in the automotive industry.  Their
i960(R) risc processor was outselling all other risc processors
combined.  And of course they were still making the fastest x86
compatable processor.  Around that same time, as I recall, DEC and
IBM laid off a number of people approximately equal to the entire
Intel corporate workforce.

Correctly recognizing the trend toward home computing, Intel continued
to invest heavily in fab capacity to insure it's ability to supply an
amazing number of microprocessors to the world.  Again, entrepreneurial
risk taking at its best.  And, needless to say, Intel continued to
build the fastest available x86 compatible processors.  Given all the
above, I think Intel was entitled to brag a little about the fact that
most computers did have Intel Inside.

