| I'm not too familiar with the US legal system, but did anyone else
| notice that the text of the lawsuit ends with "Plaintiff demands trial 
| by jury on all issues." Is that normal, and what are the implications? 
| Anyone?

Under US law, the defendent in a criminal case, or either side in a
civil case, may demand trial by jury.  The sole exception is for very
"small" matters - e.g., minor criminal infractions where the only
penalty is a fine less than some amount.  In a trial by jury, nominally,
the jury decides the facts, and the judge's role is to run the courtroom
and instruct the jury on the relevant law.  The alternative is a trial
before a judge alone; the judge then decides the facts himself.

I believe most trials in the US are jury trials; lawyers feel they have
more strategies available to them.  (Of course, their opponents have
more strategies available as well.  Jury trials are generally less
predictable.  Juries also decide the size of awards in civil cases -
though the judge reviews the results - and they are often much more
generous than a judge would be.)  Essentially, going in front of a jury
raises the stakes, and lowers the predictability, for both sides.  In a
civil case, this is generally favorable to the plaintiff:  After all,
the status quo is what is being put at risk, and the status quo favors
the defendent - else why is the plaintiff bring suit?

							-- Jerry



