| > The i432 is a prime example of Intel's poor ability to design 
| > balanced,
|
| I'd like to form my own judgements about the 432.
| What's available about it?

Probably the best place to start is a retrospective by one (or more?) of
the designers.  It appeared in TOCS in the early '90's; one of the
authors was Robert Colewell (or something like that.  He went to
Multiflow after working on the 432, and used to post regularly to this
group, but I don't recall seeing anything from him in a couple of
years.)  The TOCS article describes the 432 and discusses many of the
tradeoffs - many incorrect in retrospect - made in the design.  The
conclusion is that, if you want it, you can get true, secure capability-
based hardware that can support very fine-grained protection.  It'll
cost you about a factor of 2 in raw speed - or would have, with the
technologies available at the time.  (It's hard to judge how recent
trends might affect this.)  The 432 didn't come anywhere close to a
factor of 2 slowdown - probably more like a factor of 20 - but the
reasons for that were incidental, not fundamental.

							-- Jerry



