From: tcmay@got.net (Tim May)
Subject: Intel Basic and Applied Research (and the DEC lawsuit)
Date: 1997/05/14
Message-ID: <tcmay-ya023180001405971414270001@news.znet.com>
X-Deja-AN: 241567967
References: <5lbm3p$qgn$1@nnrp1.crl.com> <5lcp39$t1n@ultranews.duc.auburn.edu>
Organization: None
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,comp.sys.intel,comp.arch,sci.electronics



Caveat: I was at Intel from 1974 to 1986 and saw many of these events
firsthand. Also, I was hired by Craig Barrett, currently COO of Intel, and
worked for him from '74 to '79. I may be somewhat biased. And I still have
a lot of Intel stock, so I am certainly biased in hoping Intel continues to
prosper. However, I use a Macintosh, and have since 1985, so  I'm not
completely blinded. I attended many of the Homebrew Computer Club meetings,
circa 1976-79, so I also tracked what was happening with CP/M, MITS-Basic,
Microsoft, etc. To further confuse things, I bought one of the first VAXes
for Intel in 1979. These are to let you all know a bit of my biases and
viewpoints.

In article <5lcp39$t1n@ultranews.duc.auburn.edu>, stuttjc@eng.auburn.edu wrote:

> In article <5lbm3p$qgn$1@nnrp1.crl.com>, Subir Grewal
<subir@crl3.crl.com> writes:

> >think Intel as such is a revolutionary company, it was just able
> >to arbitrage it's position into a very lucrative business.  DEC on the
> >other hand has been phenomenally influential in the development of
> >computing, right from when Digital's minis began to cut into the
> >mainframe business and time-sharing slowly destroyed batch processing. 
> >
> 
> Intel did invent the concept of the single-chip microprocessor, the 4004. 
> Maybe they should sue everyone else. :)  

Not just this, but here is a partial list of Intel's inventions/developments:

* the first MOS RAM to compete with core memory in a big way (the 1101 and
then the 1103).

* the microprocessor and microcontroller (to the extent there's a
difference), including the 4004, 8008, 8080, and later processors. (And the
iAPX 432, object-oriented architecture, which was a market failure.)

* the industry's first computer-aided design tools (this is widely
unappreciated, but was instrumental to Intel's ability to do design
verification, continuity checking, circuit analysis, and mask generation)

* dynamic RAM (IBM also did influential work on this in the late 60s, esp.
Bob Dennard and his researchers. But Intel really built the first dynamic
RAMs.)

* erasable programmable read-only memories, EPROMs

* microcomputer languages (Gary Kildall was contracted for this by Intel.)
And the microcomputer development system (MDS).

(Any of these developments had influences, and even parallel inventions,
from other places. But any objective analysis of Intel's history would
_have_ to conclude that these developments were largely Intel's
achievements, and that they revolutionized the computer industry.)

Intel also was a pioneer in object-oriented computing. (Not the inventor,
as that belongs to many, including Simula, Smalltalk, Alan Kay, etc..)

(By the way, though the "432" turned out to be a market failure, I can tell
you that Intel did a huge amount of computer architecture and chip research
on this, ranging from work on caches,TLBs, barrel shifters, etc., to a new
circuit process ("X-MOS"), to new packaging, to Ada and operating system
development.  Oh, and impressive work on fault tolerance (one of the most
impressive demos I ever saw was in 1981-2, when an Intel Gemini system
running four 432 processors was subjected to "hot removal" of its various
processor boards...the Unix process slowed down, but never hiccuped. Try
_that_ today!) . When DEC claims Intel has done no basic research, bear in
mind the billion or so dollars Intel spent on this project. That it failed
is beside the point.)

And so on. I could cite dozens of areas where Intel pioneered basic work. 
(I could even cite my own reasearch, and why IBM and Bell Labs considered
it so important. But I digress.)

The context of Grove's and Barrett's comments about Intel now having to do
basic research sound damning, granted. But how offhand comments _sound_ and
what reality really is are often two different things. They may have meant
that Intel would have to step up its R & D efforts even more, or they may
have been feeling good about Intel's successes and just (understandably)
bragging a bit. In any case, the facts will all come out.

The notion that Intel "stole" its success from DEC, or Cyrix, like some
kind of high tech Prometheus, is absurd. Intel didn't do it all, of course,
nor was I always happy with Intel's R & D budget, but I can assure you all
that Intel was always solidly grounded in science (the top three leaders,
Noyce, Moore, and Grove, were all Ph.D. scientists, with Noyce the
co-inventor of the IC, Moore the leading visionary of VLSI ("Moore's law"),
and Grove the leading device physicist of the 1960s (author of "Physics and
Technology of Semiconductor Devices," the leading textbook of its day, and
still a classic).

Bear this all in mind when claims are made that the DEC lawsuit proves that
Intel stole its technology. All it proves is that DEC has filed a lawsuit.

Maybe I'll write a longer version of this article over the coming weeks.
Since current Intel employees are pretty much urged not to comment
publically in fora like this, the bashing of Intel is everlasting and
vicious. (Not that Intel doesn't deserve criticism, and nobody, certainly
not me, is suggesting censorship!)

Not having worked for Intel since '86, I'm free to comment.

--Tim May

