no_spam@Glue.umd.edu (David T. Wang) writes:

>These patents appear wonderfully vague.  Perhaps I am simply out of my 
>league in the subtleties of modern microprocessor architecture, but
>I simply can't tell how anyone can build a modern microprocessor without
>some sort of branch prediction, yet clear as day, there is a patent granted
>to Digital on Branch Prediction.

Patents are always "wonderfully vague" (nice phrase ;-) by intent and
tradition.  They always try to cover as much territory as possible,
while not specifying any more detail than necessary.  If you specify a
detail, somebody would find another implementation.  If you don't 
specify, you cover all possible ways of doing something (you hope).


