H.W. Stockman wrote:
> 
> Douglas Borsom wrote:
> 
> > The press release states the "patents relate to technologies--cache
> > management, branch prediction and high-speed instruction processing"
> > not that the patents are for the technologies themselves. As you
> > imply, without specifics, it's impossible to say.
> 
> Didn't the first alpha -- 21066 --
> have a primitive direct-mapped L1 cache?
> The Pentium cache alone was more advanced?

  Why don't you read up on cache design before calling direct mapped
  cache primitive.  For many chip designs/code trace combos the performance
  advantage from reducing the L1 miss rate from greater associativity
  doesn't make up for the performance loss from clock rate reduction.  The
  technology trends towards larger L1s and higher clock rates makes direct
  mapping desirable under more and more circumstances.

  BTW, the P5 cache was unified while all Alphas use Harvard architecture
  (split instruction/data) L1.  Which choice would you call more primitive?
  Also, P6 (PPro and PII) uses direct mapped Harvard L1s.  Would you say the
  P6 is more primitive than P5?

