Of course Digital isn't happy about the possibility of not being able to
sell PII PCs in the future.  Intel has created through their marketing
an image of quality that consumers are responding to.  And this image
has created fantastic profits for many computer manufacturers using the
Pentium chip, DEC included.  The element of irony and contradiction
within this whole episode, however, lies in the fact that DEC's
aforementioned profitability results in part from Intel's theft of
Digital technology.

Digital is in an awkward position.  Intel uses intellectual property
developed by Digital, yet pays nothing for such use (goes the
allegation).  Yet Digital profits from Intel's thievery precisely
because they sell PCs using the Pentium chip.

I can only speculate, but I suppose DEC played this game for a while,
calculating that the profits they realized from sales of Pentium-based
systems at least somewhat covered the unrealized revenue re: Intel and
its non-payment for technology developed by Digital. Moreover, DEC
received a free-ride on the coat-tails of Intel's incredible yet
extremely-expensive "Intel-Inside" advertising campaign.  Hence, the
complicated picture that is beginning to emerge is that Intel used in
its Pentium line technology developed by Digital (loss of revenue to
DEC).  Yet, Intel itself spent enormous sums of money advertising the
resulting Pentium line.  Demand for Digital systems using the Pentium
line of chips thus increased precisely because of Intel's incredible
marketing scheme (increase of revenue to DEC).  Digital provided the
technology and Intel provided the sales technique, perhaps leading to
tacit cooperation among DEC and Intel, the results of which were
mutually-beneficial, leading to, at least temporarily, increased profits
for both Intel and DEC.      

For whatever complicated reasons, however, DEC has decided to pull out
of this little game.  And you are entirely right, DEC _might_ be pushed
in a negative way by its decision to take on Intel.  Given past
precedent, Intel might try as hard as it can to punish DEC for not
bowing down and accepting Intel's monopoly position in the chip market
(attained in part through use of Digital technology).  But to use your
language: "What exactly is your point?"  Yes, Digital _might_ pay for
challenging Intel.  There is nothing new here.

That Digital does have a product superior to anything Intel can now
offer, however, is undoubtedly playing a role in its decision to
"challenge" Intel.  The Alpha chip is an alternative to the PPro, does
run x86 apps at least as fast as the PPro, and will begin to be pushed
into the mainstream market.  That has been the plan of DEC for some time
now: push the new generations of the Alpha into the mainstream,
challenging the monopoly now held by Intel in the chip market.  (What
Digital doesn't have is the "Intel-Inside" slogan, which, of course,
should not be under-estimated). 

Moreover, Digital does have access to the K6, which runs as fast as the
PII under Windows 95.  So DEC has its Alpha for high-end systems and the
K6 for home systems.  Would DEC like to also use the PII?  Of course
they would.  Yet something is pushing them in another direction. 
 
> As nice as the Alpha is, it's a noncompetitorin the business world, and
> even in various specialized markets the Pentium-type processors are
> outselling the Alpha by a wide margin.
> 

See above.

> If DEC abandons the Pentium market, or will no longer be able to get
> assured supplies of Pentiums, the effects on their bottom line will be
> severe. No doubt they'll then try to charge Intel with anti-competitive
> practices for failing to ship to them chips they claim are infringing.
> 


Yawn. Maybe a better reaction on the part of DEC would be using Intel
technology for future chips (if such technology even exists :> ).     


> Where I come from, Intel would be doing the best thing it could do to say:
> "OK, you allege this is an infringing chip. If follows that we should not
> be shipping them to you, as you will add them to your list of claimed
> damages. Effective immediately, and continuing until this matter is
> resolved completely, we will not ship to you any chips you claim are
> infringing."
> 

Perhaps that's why you don't run a Fortune 200 company.  The
relationship shared by Intel and Digital is complex, and one that can
not be reduced in a way that you would seem to like.

I doubt Intel wants to lose Digital as a customer.  DEC no doubt is one
of the major buyers of the Pentium chip; Intel would stand to lose as
much as DEC if shipments of Pentiums were to stop flowing to DEC.  And
not just in terms of lost sales to DEC;  Intel can only threaten so many
companies before such tactics begin to backfire on them.

Intel realizes it is no longer the only player in the chip market.  AMD
has the K6.  Cyrix will soon have the M2.  DEC has the Alpha.  Right
now, the last thing Intel wants to do is alienate any more major players
in this little computer-chip game.
 

I must admit that this is an intriguing development in the chip/PC
market, however.  DEC really is taking a risk in challenging Intel
(which I think is more or less your point, which I agree with).  I doubt
DEC is doing this for reasons of simple patent violations, however. 
Digital, armed with its new generations of the Alpha chip and AMD's K6,
sees a chance to grab market share from Intel in the chip market.  The
lawsuit, if effective, would hinder Intel's ability to sell its Pentium
line (if only for the negative image created by the emerging
allegations), allowing Digital to increase sales of its new generations
of Alpha chips.  Moreover, Digital can use the K6 in home PC systems if
need be.

If nothing else, the aforementioned precedings show that Intel is not
the only ruthless producer and marketer of PC chips.  It seems as if
Intel and Digital aren't as differnet in their methods as some might
think.

If DEC and Intel do break off their amicable relationship, however, one
does have to wonder: where will Intel get its new technology?

Advertising campaigns can only go so far, after all. 

      


