[ Gerald A. Feldman ]

>>  First of all, from looking at the code, it does not appear to be a code
>>  generation bug at all. It is only a bug if the code beyond the && would
>>  cause a side effect, such as invoke a function, modify a variable, or
>>  cause an exception.

[ Roy Smith ]

>>  That's like saying that if the code generated for "x = 3" actually assigns
>>  the value 4 to x, it's only a bug if the value of x is used later in the
>>  program.

As a parenthetical and somewhat off-topic aside, I'd leave *that* one for the
philosophers, as a code-generator analogue of "if a tree falls in a forest ..."
sort of question.  I *have* encountered compilers which don't bother assigning
any value at all to a variable if they can determine with 100% certainty that
the variable is never used subsequent to the requested assignment.

However, to get back on-track ...

[ Roy Smith ]

>>  The language semantics say that in "x && y", y is not evaluated unless x is
>>  true.  This lets you do things like "xp && *xp".  Or "is_valid(foo) &&
>>  get_value(foo)".  Or "x < 3 && x++".  Some of those may be ugly, but they
>>  are legal, and all require that the part after the && not be evaluated if
>>  the part before the && is false.

Roy is spot-on correct on this point.  (Hey, I *have* to agree; I'm the one who
started this whole thread! ;-)  When in doubt, consult scripture:  K&R makes it
totally clear, when it says

	Expressions connected by && or || are evaluated left to right,
	and it is guaranteed that evaluation will stop as soon as the
	truth or falsehood is known.

QED.  Bug.  Case closed.

