[snip]

The C standard explicitly forbids the execution of the rhs expression of
the && operator, if the lhs expression if false. Writing

x && 1/x

should never cause a divide-by-zero exception (or the equivalent an
Alpha's.)

If the DEC compiler does execute 1/x, even if x is zero, it's broken.

