In article <rnsp00lsgl.fsf@michael.huygens.org>, dillema@cs.utwente.nl (Feico Dillema) writes:
> In article <862605980.5955@dejanews.com> antonr@erols.com writes:
> 
>    I have several questions about Multia box.
> 
>    1. What is the possibility of using SCSI drive besides the 2.5" one? Has
>    anyone been able to fit 3.5 inch 1" height hard drive inside Multi? What
>    about power for the drive. Also, anybody been successful with removable
>    SCSI drives such as IOMEGA Zip?
> 
> I've heard adding an internal drive may give power or cooling
> problems, and there does not seem an obvious way to add one (I thought
> of replacing the internal diskdrive with another 2.5" hardisk). I've
> added an external SCSI harddisk and an external SCSI IOMEGA ZIP. Both
> work fine on my UDB running NetBSD.
> 
You can also replace the 2.5" scsi disk with a 2.5" ide disk (Mine now has a
2gb 2.5" ide disk installed). If you look at the motherboard just opposite
where the 2.5" disk's connector is, there is a 44 pin header.
This is the IDE controller connector. You will need a jumper to connect this
header to the disk. Note that this header and the connector on the 2.5"
disk are NOT the standard IDE (40 pin) connector. THis is a 44 pin connector
which includes power and the pins are closer together found on 2.5" ide drives.
You will also have to pre-partition the drive (I used a standard to 2.5" adapter
and connected the drive to a normal pc to partition it). I then installed the
drive in place of the scsi drive. You "can" use the scsi cable to connect
the IDE drive although it is a little too wide and hits another connector
(the floppy connector?) but it will work. The proper cable is what is used in
notebooks to connect the 2.5" drive to the notebook connector. There are a few
other steps you have to go through like telling the system that there is a new
system partition (and letting it init it). I was then able to install WNT 4.0 from
an external cd.

The 2.5" ide drive cable  (this is a short 1-2" long "jumper") to connect the
drive to the motherboard and the 3.5" ide to 2.5" ide adapter are available
from Computer & electronic Solutions in Redford, MI (313) 532-4291.
I think the cable was about $10 and the adapter about $8. I have no relationship
to the company other than being a satisfied customer.

As I said above I have done this with a 2gb 2.5" drive. With the 2gb internal, I don't
NEED to have an external drive hanging off the system and I have seen 2.5" drives as large
as 3gb. The largest scsi 2.5" drive I have seen is 800MB so IDE is the way to go.

Ronald.Schubot@wmich.edu
Systems Manager
Western Michigan University

p.s. My Multia is a model VX42-F2, this may not work on the VX41 or VX40



