Aandi Inston (quite@dial.pipex.com) wrote:
: wade@sisko.asub.arknet.edu wrote:
: 
: >I am trying to use a DEC LN03R Scriptprinter with WFW3.11.  This is a 
: >PS level 1 printer.  When I print a doc with any fonts other than the
: >ones in the printer, they do not get sent but are substituted with 
: >ones in the printer.  The same doc will print fine on my level 2 
: >declaser.  Is this a limitation of PS level 1 or am I missing something
: >to get the fonts to download?
: 
: If the printer is a non-Adobe clone older than 1980, it won't
: understand type 1 fonts, the only common type in use now.

Hi, there, and I think we're talking about non-Adobe-licensed printers up
to about 1990, when the Type 1 font format was "declassified" by Adobe
<grin>.

Just to clarify the part of the question about Level 1 and Level 2: the
ability to print Type 1 fonts is a separate issue having to do with the
fact that the Type 1 format was originally proprietary, and was meant to
print _only_ on Adobe-licensed interpreters. Other printers of the time
used the open "user-defined" Type 3 format.

A major advantage of Type 1 is hinting, or fine-tuning of font outlines
for lower resolution devices. Below around 600 dpi, such fine adjustments
can greatly improve type quality; and during the pre-1990 era a 300 dpi
resolution was standard for desktop laser printers.

Because of market factors, Adobe decided in 1989 to make its Type 1
standard open, and naturally printer manufacturers incorporated it into
their PostScript-compatible machines. It happens that PostScript Level 2
was becoming the new standard around the same time; Adobe's Second Edition
of the _PostScript Language Reference Manual_, defining the Level 2
standard, came out in 1990.

Thus any PS Level 2 device, as well as any Adobe-licensed PS Level 1
device, is likely to support Type 1 fonts. 

A possible solution does occur to me for users of pre-1990 "clone" 
printers (assuming a firmware upgrade isn't available). I'm pretty sure
that there is software out there than can convert a Type 1 font to Type 3
(user-defined), which any PostScript interpreter should be able to handle. 

I say this because I remember that sometime around 1993, a helpful person
converted a freeware Type 1 font to Type 3 so that I could use it with an
interpreter which had limited support for the Type 1 format. Maybe other
people could name specific programs that do this for various platforms.

Most respectfully,
Margo Schulter
mschulter@value.net

