>}Someone just called my attention to a really disturbing
>}bug in DEC's C compiler.  It seems that if a program is
>}compiled with "-O", then evaluation of "expr1 && expr2"
>}is botched:  Even when expr1 evaluates to 0, expr2 gets
>}evaluated anyway!
>}
>}The implications of this bug are rather staggering; for
>}example, what if expr2 has side-effects?  Gack.

Any compiler code generation bug is "really disturbing".  This has to be any
programmer's worst nightmare.  The thought of doing any kind of software
developent on a compiler that isn't doing 100% rock-solid code generation
gives me the willies.  I can live with slow code.  I can live with missing
language features.  I can live with parsers unable to handle arbitrarily
complex expressions.  But if it compiles, I have to *know* it compiled
correctly.

We're running DU-4.0B on our new alpha boxes.  Is this a problem in the
compiler that ships with that system?  Should I be pointing my ftp
connection at MIT and downloading gcc if I want to be able to sleep nights?

