>Any compiler code generation bug is "really disturbing".  This has to be
any
>programmer's worst nightmare.  The thought of doing any kind of software
>developent on a compiler that isn't doing 100% rock-solid code generation
>gives me the willies.  I can live with slow code.  I can live with
missing
>language features.  I can live with parsers unable to handle arbitrarily
>complex expressions.  But if it compiles, I have to *know* it compiled
>correctly.


Sorry, I don't buy this. If you're serious about QA, you put release
versions through their paces just like you put debug versions. People don't
do line-by-line proofs that their million-line programs are bug-free---they
do black-box testing. As such, bugs caused by compiler errors should be
treated the same way as bugs in your code.

Now that's not to say that you should ignore compiler quality, of
course---but an occasional compiler bug is part of the game.







