> 
> I have no idea why so many people here have this obsession with
> `at the same clock speed,' anyway. The Alpha is a RISC architecture
> that does less per clock cycle than the CISC-based Pentium
> architecture. Part of the whole point of the Alpha is that it can
> overcome this by having a lot more clock cycles than a Pentium:
> there's a very good reason why Digital is shipping 600 MHz Alpha
> CPUs when Intel is barely even testing 300 MHz Pentium-type chips.
> 

LMAO...  These benchmark figures have no relation to the real world. 
Although there is little doubt a CISC computer does more per clock cycle
than a RISC one, wether or not more work is accomplished is debatable.  

Intel does not have a processor running at more than 233MHz commercially
available because they do not yet have the technology to produce one. 
While on the subject of 'Intel's chips are CISC so they do more per
clock cycle...' it should also be noted that Intel's chips are 32bit. 
Alpha is 64bit.  Hmmm...

Another thing to be wary of is performance/cost benchmarks.  Where cheap
computers appear to outperform the more expensive ones.  They don't. 
The extra performance just costs more. 

Now, as I said, benchmarks bear no relation to actual performance, the
kind you see if you purchase the machine and use it.  In my experience
of NT servers, it takes 4 Pentium Pro 200 Mhz machines to perform the
workload of an Alpha 4100 with a single 300Mhz cpu.  

With the Alpha you also have the option of adding a further 3 cpus,
and/or upgrading them to 633Mhz, with 1GHz on the way.  With the Pentium
Pros you can expect to replace them with new machines within 2 years. 
You don't have to take my word for this, if you have $100000 you can
experience it first hand.

NT5 is set to be 64bit.  DEC already produce a 64bit version of UNIX. 
The current Intel machines can run neither.   

To summarise:

1 Alpha 4100 with single 300Mhz CPU will do the work of four 200MHz
Pentium Pro machines.  The alpha can be upgraded and should be good for
5 years.  The Pentiums can't.  

This is based on my colleague has 4 200MHz Pentium Pro's, and I have an
Alpha 4100, running comparible networks, and my mine goes faster than
his.  

Hows that?

Craig

PS  Alpha had 128Mb of memory at that time.  Entire machine has since
been upgraded (extra CPU, more memory), and a cluster machine is
forthcoming.  Pentiums had 64Mb memory each.



