Sigurd Schelstraete wrote:
> 
> hello,
> I am trying to run a program (in fortran) on an alphastation 500 with
> digital UNIX 4.0A. This program previously ran on a SUN workstation
> without problems. On the alphastation, it runs for a while and then exits
> with the message:
> 
> "forrtl: error (65): floating invalid"
> "Abort - core dumped"
> 
> When I try running it with the debugger, it stops with the message
> 
> "Thread received signal FPE".
> 
> Since I have no idea how to proceed from there, I would appreciate any
> hints as to what may cause this error message.

This means what it says. Your program generated a floating point trap,
called SIGFPE. That could mean a number of things, but either you're
doing something "bad" with IEEE floating point operations that SUN
(SPARC?) didn't notice, or you're doing something that Alpha doesn't
support. (Although IEEE floating point is a standard, every
implementation has its share of "quirks".)

When you have the program sitting at the prompt, you'll need to see what
the code is doing around that point to figure out why it got the SIGFPE.

