In article <3370608F.5EE3@shef.ac.uk>, S Sotin <ZZC96SS@shef.ac.uk> writes:
:To help people with language disorders, I am creating a software. For 
:this, I need to record on computer, sentences with real voice (under 
:Windows 3.xx). Afterwards, I need to detect want a specified word is 
:pronunced. 
:Example: target : elephant
:	 sentence : in the jungle the elephant is charging the hunter
:		
:When the target is pronunced, I must be able to receive a signal or 
:somthing like that to start a program.			      |
:Both target and sentence would be recorded. Which kind of tools can I use 
:to do this? 	

   Assuming you do not wish to perform isolated or continuous word speech
   recognition -- these are difficult tasks at best -- but rather wish to
   simply record (digitize) sound and later play it back, then most any
   multi-media software package and basic multi-media hardware can be used.
   Digitized processing is fairly inexpensive, and relatively available.

   OpenVMS and UNIX offer multi-media software packages, as do a number of
   PC-based software vendors.

   If, on the other hand, you wish to recognize the word "elephant" when
   it is pronounced in a continuous sentence, then you have few (or no)
   options -- this is beyond the capabilities of most (all?) present-day
   voice-recognition systems, particularly given your stated requirement
   for processing input from folks with language disorders.  (There are
   advanced research projects actively working on speech recognition and
   on the associated processing algorithms...)

   Isolated-word speech recognition isn't quite as difficult -- but most
   packages have a limited vocabulary, or speaker dependencies, or both.
   Packages such as DIGITAL's DECvoice (hardware and software) are quite
   capable of isolated-word recognition, and support both programmable
   (speaker-dependent) and speaker-independent isolated-word vocabularies.

   Recognition is fairly expensive, and relatively unavailable.

