The following is a commentary from the May 5, 1997 issue of Shannon
Knows DEC. The light at the end of the OpenVMS tunnel just may **not**
be the headlamp of an oncoming locomotive, after all...



WHITHER OPENVMS?

(c) 1997 by Terry C. Shannon

There's good news and not-so-good news on the OpenVMS front. First, the
bad news: as reported last month in SKD, DEC quietly boosted the price
of VMScluster software. DEC's justification for the final increment of a
four-step OpenVMS revenue enhancement strategy involved pricing
VMScluster software at a 5 percent premium vis-a-vis HP's Unix cluster
offering. Go figure.


SKD already has done some figuring. Last spring, we went through a
year's worth of Digital price books and found that the firm boosted
OpenVMS operating system, cluster, and applications license prices by 35
to 45 percent during the preceding twelve months. OpenVMS bracket creep
continued apace with the January 1997 "Mother of All Alpha Price Cuts."
Once again, an OpenVMS and Digital UNIX price disparity-albeit a modest
one-reared its ugly head.


Things have been even uglier on the Internet. Constant Readers of
newsgroups such as comp.os.vms undoubtedly are aware of the "Whither
OpenVMS" threads that appear on a regular basis. Those who contribute to
these threads share a basic philosophy: Digital is killing OpenVMS. And
truth be told, these OS loyalists can cite enough proof points to ensure
that they're not dismissed as conspiracy theorists.


Despite Internet newsgroup angst and what SKD hopes is the final chapter
in the OpenVMS Bracket Creep saga, DEC continues to attract new OpenVMS
customers. Underscoring this fact were DEC's 3FQ97 financial results:
Digital UNIX revenues headed south, but the OpenVMS server business
grew. Again.


DEC's OpenVMS focus clearly is on high-end-and high-margin-
opportunities. The recently formed Windows NT and OpenVMS Systems Group
reflects the "unlimited high-end" strategy So does the nascent OpenVMS
Galaxies Software Architecture. Now fully funded, the the little-known
Galaxies program is designed to substantially upgrade OpenVMS so the OS
can support humongous "clusters in a cabinet," e.g. aggregations of up
to 256 CPU nodes divided into multiple SMP partitions. DEC's answer to
the Cray-inspired Sun Starfire server is sure to eclipse the
capabilities of Sun's new Ultra Enterprise 10000. We'll revisit Galaxies
and the corequisite large-SMP Wildfire enterprise platform at an
appropriate time.


In the interim, Good Things finally are happening at the very low end.
Today's announcement of Digital UNIX support on heretofore WNT-only
Personal Workstations appears to have no direct impact on OpenVMS, but
the concurrent price reductions of up to one-third on OS-agnostic
AlphaStations will benefit the OpenVMS constituency. Moreover, OpenVMS
support is all but certain to come to Alpha PWs. Stay tuned, SKD expects
to see affordable desktop OpenVMS systems by the end of the year!

Terry C. Shannon
