>>I would like to 
>>know what makes the engineering effect to get Linux working on these machines
>>not worthwhile....
>
>Perhaps because it's a larger effort that it appears? It appears
>to me that the main problem is that the Linux kernel is designed
>around PC hardware. You'll note that all of the ports that work
>well (with the notable exception of the Sparc and the amiga) are
>running on hardware that is essentially a PC with a different
>processor. (`Linux: turning workstations into PCs.' :-)) There's

NetBSD...you want software?  Compile it all yourself.  :-)

(That's what all *real* men do anyway.  ;-)

>not really much structure in place to make it easy to add new
>busses, or to make drivers bus-independent.

The SBUS and PCI seem to be the only ones that *matter* for
mainstream use.  The core developers just don't care that much
themselves if Linux runs on every piece of hardware out there.
And if Linux isn't as bus-portable, fine.  I really didn't
hear Dave Miller screaming much when he did the SPARC port.

>But you can always run NetBSD on your old Turbochannel Alphas. And
>on your Turbochannel DECStations, too.

Yep, and that's exactly what I would encourage people to do.
The NetBSD and Linux camps have different objectives and goals,
and that's fine.  I commend them for wanting to get NetBSD
running on those machines.  The core design of NetBSD may lend
itself a little better than Linux does to being ported to a
new complete architecture.  At then end of the day, though,
I don't think it matters one bit.  The amount of *extra* work
you'd have to do to port Linux to most of those machines is
negligible compared to the entire job.  So, whether it gets 
done depends on what the person who a) owns the hardware and
b) has the knowledge to do a kernel port (or the capacity to
learn) and c) what *his* preference is.

I still wish I had time to try NetBSD on my Sun 3/60...but
there is a Linux port in progress, so my motivation is to 
wait.  :-)


--Donnie

