Peter Desnoyers (pjd@giga-net.com) wrote:
: On 1 May 1997 13:10:34 GMT, torbenm@diku.dk (Torben AEgidius Mogensen)
: wrote:

: >pjd@giga-net.com (Peter Desnoyers) writes:
: >
: >>Of course, back then there were all the people regretting that they
: >>had killed the ARM-based Apple II follow-on, because in native mode it
: >>was faster than the much more expensive Mac II.  I'm a lot more

[...]

: It was called "Mobius", and a number of engineers in ATG still had
: prototypes (without cases :-) on their desks in '88 or '89.  They had
: developed a pretty robust operating system to run on it (the Apple
: Real-time Kernel, no relation to ROSE), and could emulate the Apple II
: on top of it at many times the speed of a 6502.

I have heard about this before, but "Mobius" was given, in my now
unremembered source, as the codename for the legendary ARM-based
Macintosh. This source mentioned it in the context of Apple's
enthusiasm for the ARM and their part ownership of ARM Ltd. I remember
that the 88K series was also mentioned.

It could have been one of the British computing magazines which
printed such "folklore", and it would have been likely that they would
have referred to the Macintosh rather than the Apple II series, since
Apple really made its name over in the UK with the Mac, and I don't
think the IIGS ever really sold there at all.

: The neat thing about the ARM was that it was designed in the first
: place to emulate a 6502 for the second-generation BBC micro.

The place of the ARM in the history of Acorn certainly does fit it to
the plans for faster BBC micros, as it is believed that Acorn were
disappointed with the performance of CPU's at the time, such as the
NS32016 (used in the Cambridge Workstation). However, myth and legend
enter the picture as far as the ambitious plans for the ARM were
concerned, with the abandoned ARX operating system project.

I suppose that it would be really interesting to chart all these
abandoned projects and technologies, and the effect that they had,
or rather that their abandonment had on the companies which might
have released or continued them.

--
