>on top of it at many times the speed of a 6502.

>The neat thing about the ARM was that it was designed in the first
>place to emulate a 6502 for the second-generation BBC micro.

This was not the only purpose, but it was one of the design goals that
it should be able to emulate a BBC micro (2MHz 6502) at a speed
comparable to the 'real thing'. This was achieved with the 8MHz ARM2
processor used in the 'Archimedes' line of computers. The ARM3 (at up
to 36 MHz) was available in 1989, and would have been far faster than
a Mac II. I think even the 8MHz ARM would outperform a Mac II, so it
was probbaly the one used in the Mobius (at least the '88 version).

Why is it that marketing people kill products that they think may
compete with existing lines? While this imay be true, they forget that
this will also make the new product compete with other companies
lines, and that there is no reason another company can't come up with
a similar product, and hence create the competition anyway, with the
difference that it is that other company that reaps the profits. All
cases that I have heard of where this has happened are later on
perceived as mistakes.

Furthermore, I can't really see the Mobius as competing directly with
the Mac. After all, what has sold the Mac has not been superior
performance but superior software. The Mobius would have appealed to
a different audience, and would have been more likely to cut into the
DOS PC market than the Mac market.

So, it would indeed have been interesting to know what would have
happened if Apple had produced the Mobius. It could well have been a
cash-cow, selling into a market where the Mac would have been to
expensive.

In the discussion of which processor Apple chose for the new Mac, it
was stated that a requirement was 80-bit FP. The ARM FP instruction
set has always supported 80-bit FP and at the time in question a
hardware FPU (FPA-10) was avaialble for the 25MHz ARM3. Hence, ARM
would not have been a bad choice for Apple. It is true that ARM hasn't
had as fast processors as other mass-market CPUs (until the StrongARM,
at least), but this has mainly been for lack of a perceived market. If
a market the size of the Apple Macintosh market was present, faster
versions of ARM (including FP) would have been available long before.
After all, the development of the PPC has mainly been driven by Apples
use, at least after IBM cancelled their plans of using it for a
mass-market machine.

It is true that ARM doesn't support unaligned access (which was
mentioned as another requirement), but this would have been easy to
rectify, just as bi-endianess was added to the ARM6 when Apple
required it and 16-bit load/store was added in StrongARM and
Thumb-extended ARM7's.

	Torben Mogensen (torbenm@diku.dk)



