The story I had heard was that when the product was demo-ed to the
appropriate high level executive, who saw how fast it was (this was circa
the Mac II) he told them to bury it in as deep a hole as they could find. 

I think at this point Apple had decided that they were going to unify
around a single architecture (the Mac).  They intended to move everyone
(education markets included) over to the Mac, and so the Apple II was
going to be dumped

They didn't want anything around that might cause the life (and therefore
support costs) of the Apple II family to continue. Hence the decision.

I believe those are the simple reasons- there are probably other reasons,
on both sides, why the decision was made as it was- these things are
rarely all that simple, however thye hindsight.

Apple has had sort of an unrequited love-love relationship with the ARM
architecture for quite a while. Mobius wasn't the only project that an ARM
processor was designed for, and the the ARM had been modified at Apple's
request at least once (the big/little endian bit) prior to the Newton MMU,
which finally made it into production.

