>	Donnie> Or just find a Linux vendor if that's what you want.
>	Donnie> There are several, and there are some who *only* sell
>	Donnie> Linux hardware.  Poke about and you will find...
>
>But the Linux hardware vendors typically don't offer the same
>price/service combination as the established vendors, who have the
>volume.
>
>Buying a pre-installed Linux system is certainly more expensive than
>buying one with Win* on it and overlaying it with Linux.

Sorry, but I disagree.

The people building the Linux machines *know* what works with
Linux.  They know what works best.  They know what their customers
want in a *nix machine.  They are best equipped to make sure you
get something that works.  That Windows machine you buy could
end up with some funky video card that won't work.  Gateway sold
a good many machines with "Matrox Millenium" cards in them.  Those
cards didn't even work with commercial X servers for a while because
they were "special" Millenium's that were made for Gateway.  So,
you can't trust anything other than "windows will work on it".
Big deal.

The Linux hardware vendors are getting larger quickly.  The price
is *not* a big issue here.  All the vendors I've seen compete 
nicely with the larger, more "established" PeeCee vendors out there
*if* you compare line items of the components.

The key is that you get *Linux* service from them.  For instance,
you might be inclined to look on a hardware list and *think* you
want a 3c905 network card.  Let's say your friend has one in
his Linux box, too, and it works fine.  You call the Linux vendor
and you say that you want that card in your machine because it's
fast and you need fast because you are doing video conferencing.
A good Linux vendor will say "uh, you may want to consider these
DE-500's that we are using.  The 905 has some trouble under 
heavy load but the DE-500 works *great*."  Your windows vendor
won't know that, and you may not either.

I'm not saying using a Linux vendor is for everyone.  If you're
joe cool and are plugged into Linux already, spec your system and
get it at the cheapest place your conscience will allow.  If you're
not, you should consider a Linux vendor.


--Donnie

