wrote:

>pjd@giga-net.com (Peter Desnoyers) writes:
>
>>Of course, back then there were all the people regretting that they
>>had killed the ARM-based Apple II follow-on, because in native mode it
>>was faster than the much more expensive Mac II.  I'm a lot more
>>curious about what would have happened if they had released that
>>product...
>
>This is the first I have heard about such a project. Can you tell us
>more?

It was called "Mobius", and a number of engineers in ATG still had
prototypes (without cases :-) on their desks in '88 or '89.  They had
developed a pretty robust operating system to run on it (the Apple
Real-time Kernel, no relation to ROSE), and could emulate the Apple II
on top of it at many times the speed of a 6502.

The neat thing about the ARM was that it was designed in the first
place to emulate a 6502 for the second-generation BBC micro.


