We were able to reproduce the problem and are now investigating a
compiler fix. There appears to be a problem in the xemacs abbrev.c
source code, given the preprocessed file that you sent us.

Below is the (simplified) code that is causing the compiler error:

int SYNTAX_CODE (struct Lisp_Char_Table *table, int c) {
    return ((((c) < 160 ? (table)->ascii[c] :
              CHAR_TABLE_NON_ASCII_VALUE_UNSAFE (table, c))).val);
}

There appears to be a few problems with this:
   
     - the function returns "int" (in the original)
     - (table)->ascii[c] is of type "Lisp_Object"
     - CHAR_TABLE_NON_ASCII_VALUE_UNSAFE (table, c))).val
       is of type "long"

To see the a compilation error (not internal error), try:

FIX1 : Change the function to return "Lisp_Object"
       A compile error is generated for the conditional expression
       (I think this is acceptable)

FIX2 : Change the conditional expression to an if/then/else
       A compile error is generated for the returns, since the
       types do not match "int".
       (I think this is acceptable)

Note that with either of these "fixes", it will point to a
real source code problem and ultimately, the source code should
be fixed.

If you could, please send me a non-preprocessed version of this
same file/routine, so we can determine how the source code should
be changed.

Thanks,
