Major question:  Does this compiler internal error still happen if the 
configuration option `use-union-type=yes' is used?


Jeff Sullivan USG <jps@quarry.zk3.dec.com> writes:

> We were able to reproduce the problem and are now investigating a
> compiler fix. There appears to be a problem in the xemacs abbrev.c
> source code, given the preprocessed file that you sent us.

> Below is the (simplified) code that is causing the compiler error:

> int SYNTAX_CODE (struct Lisp_Char_Table *table, int c) {
>     return ((((c) < 160 ? (table)->ascii[c] :
>               CHAR_TABLE_NON_ASCII_VALUE_UNSAFE (table, c))).val);
> }

This appears to be the expansion of the macro

#define CHAR_TABLE_VALUE_UNSAFE(ct, ch)		\
  ((ch) < NUM_ASCII_CHARS			\
   ? (ct)->ascii[ch]				\
   : CHAR_TABLE_NON_ASCII_VALUE_UNSAFE (ct, ch))

in chartab.h.  NUM_ASCII_CHARS is 160 when compiled with MULE.  This
macro should return type Lisp_Object, as the ascii field is an array
of Lisp_Object,

struct Lisp_Char_Table
{
  struct lcrecord_header header;

  Lisp_Object ascii[NUM_ASCII_CHARS];

#ifdef MULE
  Lisp_Object level1[NUM_LEADING_BYTES];

#endif /* MULE */

  enum char_table_type type;

  /* stuff used for syntax tables */
  Lisp_Object mirror_table;
  Lisp_Object next_table; /* DO NOT mark through this. */
};

and the macro CHAR_TABLE_NON_ASCII_VALUE_UNSAFE similarly returns
type Lisp_Object.

INLINE Lisp_Object
CHAR_TABLE_NON_ASCII_VALUE_UNSAFE (struct Lisp_Char_Table *ct, Emchar ch)
{
  unsigned char lb = CHAR_LEADING_BYTE (ch);
  if (!CHAR_TABLE_ENTRYP ((ct)->level1[lb - MIN_LEADING_BYTE]))
    return (ct)->level1[lb - MIN_LEADING_BYTE];
  else
    return get_non_ascii_char_table_value (ct, lb, ch);
}

Lisp_Object get_non_ascii_char_table_value (struct Lisp_Char_Table *ct,
					   int leading_byte,
					   Emchar c);

> There appears to be a few problems with this:
   
>      - the function returns "int" (in the original)

Yes, but it is converted to int in syntax.h:

#define SYNTAX_CODE_UNSAFE(table, c) \
   XINT (CHAR_TABLE_VALUE_UNSAFE (table, c))

INLINE int SYNTAX_CODE (struct Lisp_Char_Table *table, Emchar c);
INLINE int
SYNTAX_CODE (struct Lisp_Char_Table *table, Emchar c)
{
  return SYNTAX_CODE_UNSAFE (table, c);
}

XINT is #defined from XREALINT in lisp.h.

#define XINT(obj) XREALINT (obj)

The implementation of XREALINT depends on whether lisp unions are
used.  The following definition is used if lisp unions are not used.

# define XREALINT(a) (((a) << (LONGBITS-VALBITS)) >> (LONGBITS-VALBITS))

>      - (table)->ascii[c] is of type "Lisp_Object"
>      - CHAR_TABLE_NON_ASCII_VALUE_UNSAFE (table, c))).val
>        is of type "long"

This may be confusion from not compiling with Lisp_Object as a union.

